WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.300
- Good morning and welcome to the STARS

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.060
25th Anniversary Celebration.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.620
My name is Sandy Chang,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.810
Associate Dean of STEM
Education at Yale college

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.420
and Director of the STARS Program.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.523
I'll be moderating today's sessions.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.020
We gathered to celebrate
STARS for the first time

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:25.870
since its inception.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:27.010
By any measure,

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:30.210
STARS has been extraordinarily successful.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.260
Women and underrepresented
students participated in STARS,

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.560
are significantly more likely
to persist in STEM majors

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:41.160
and to pursue STEM
disciplines after graduation.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:41.993
We were here

00:00:41.993 --> 00:00:45.170
for some of these extraordinary
STARS alums today.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.790
Students will also get to meet them

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.863
in the breakout sessions later on.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.680
We'll also hear from some of the leaders

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.520
who made the STARS Program possible.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:56.810
Many of these individuals

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:59.460
continue to serve this program today,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.180
a testament to their dedication

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.800
to make Yale STEM more
inclusive and equitable.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.930
President Peter Salovey,

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.940
the Chris Argyris Professor of Psychology,

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.503
will lead off the program
with a short video.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:15.613
- Hello!

00:01:15.613 --> 00:01:16.540
I'm Peter Salovey,

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.790
President of Yale university.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.870
Thank you for inviting me
to join you to celebrate

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:25.270
the 25th anniversary of STARS.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:26.610
A quarter century ago,

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.670
Professor Kurt Zilm,

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.270
and university secretary

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:34.300
and vice president for
university life, Kim Goff-Crews,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.240
recognized the need for
a program like this.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.530
Thanks to their vision,

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:40.510
hundreds of Yale students have gained

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.960
firsthand research experience and support

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.830
for navigating their majors
and building careers.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:51.900
Women and others from
underrepresented groups in science

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:56.010
have received the mentorship
and opportunities we know

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:59.173
are essential for success in STEM fields.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.880
I also want to express my
appreciation to Dean Sandy Chang

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.853
for leading STARS with such
enthusiasm and dedication.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.340
Many faculty members have
been critical partners

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.710
in this work.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:17.160
They've taught courses
and served as role models.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.990
Research shows that role
models play a powerful role

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:22.350
particularly for students
who come from backgrounds

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.823
that aren't well-represented in sciences.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:29.020
Accomplished educators
and researchers at YALE

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:30.560
like Dean Chang,

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.060
Dean Marvin Chun,

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.170
Professor Zilm and professor McGarry

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.913
have inspired students
through this program.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.340
The rewards have been great.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.580
STARS alumni have gone on to build careers

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:48.373
and make contributions to
science, technology and medicine.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.780
At Yale, welcoming students and scholars

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:56.880
from diverse backgrounds is
a university-wide priority.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.550
We know diversity is
integral to excellence,

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:04.483
fueling discovery and answering
our most urgent questions.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:06.613
As you know,

00:03:06.613 --> 00:03:09.820
based on engagement from
every part of our community,

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.440
I launched programs that
are part of the next phase

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.330
of the belonging at Yale initiative.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:19.430
These programs dive into our history,

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:22.750
assess and build on our current actions,

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.370
support members of the
university community

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:28.510
and create a stronger Yale for the future.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:30.560
They help us to continue to lead the way

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.583
in teaching and research
of the highest caliber.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:37.140
As we collaborate on this vital work,

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.350
it is especially important to foster

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:42.963
a sense of belonging
and inclusion in STEM.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.900
We've made progress in
increasing representation

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.900
from diverse groups in these fields,

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:51.790
but there is more to do.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:54.610
We know that our students today

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:57.943
are the leaders and the
mentors of tomorrow.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:02.090
And so programs like STARS are essential

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:03.970
to fulfilling Yale's mission

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:07.793
to improve the world today
and for future generations.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:12.150
Thank you to everyone
who has made this program

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.840
such a success,

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.450
and congratulations on
this wonderful milestone.

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:21.603
I look forward to the next
25 years of STARS at Yale.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:25.810
- Thank you very much President Salovey.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:29.640
Our next speaker is Marvin
Chun, Dean of Yale college,

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.560
the Richard and Colgate
Professor of Psychology

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.638
and Professor of Neuroscience.

00:04:34.638 --> 00:04:35.471
Dean Chun.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.610
- Good morning everyone.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:41.040
It's such an exciting day

00:04:42.469 --> 00:04:46.220
to be here for the 25th
anniversary of STARS.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:49.640
It is absolutely a top
priority for Yale College,

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:53.094
and as a scientist leading
a brain imaging lab,

00:04:53.094 --> 00:04:56.570
I'm showing our brain scanner behind me,

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.700
it's a program that's
very dear to my heart.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:04.150
As you just heard STARS really exemplifies

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:07.160
President Salovey's mission for Yale,

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.710
to be their research
university most committed

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:10.960
to teaching and learning.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:15.440
And we're also really grateful
to university secretary

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:19.210
and vice president for
student life, Kim Goff-Crews,

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:20.950
who not only, you know,

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.550
who leads our diversity, equity, inclusion

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.720
and belonging initiatives,

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:28.580
that is a major priority for Yale.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:33.580
I really celebrate her for
founding this STARS Program

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.830
along with Professor Kurt Zilm

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:38.883
and Professor Kenneth Nelson.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.460
And I also, you know,

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.370
I'm grateful for this opportunity to thank

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:50.370
and to recognize Dr. Sandy
Chan, Professor Sandy Chang.

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:53.560
He is truly passionate and tireless

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:55.493
in his leadership of STARS.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:59.560
He thinks about STARS all the time

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.780
and you really cannot ask
for a better colleague,

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.150
a better associate Dean of Science

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.410
and quantitative reasoning education.

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:10.730
You know, as an accomplished MD, PhD,

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:13.730
clinician and scientist himself,

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:16.721
and as a Yale alum, a Yale college alum,

00:06:16.721 --> 00:06:19.150
I think he really embodies

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:22.340
what we're trying to
achieve with this program

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:26.130
even as he says one of his motivations

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:27.770
for leading this program

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:30.470
is that he did not get to benefit

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.123
from something like this himself.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:38.010
I really also wanna thank
our fabulous colleagues

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.530
and faculty for teaching in this program

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:44.040
and for mentoring students in your labs.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:47.770
You know you are the engine
that really make this possible

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:49.490
and make it successful.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:52.870
And of course I applaud the students.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:55.460
Many of you who are here as alumni,

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:57.930
again serving as role models,

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:01.240
but it's really your
engagement with the program.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:05.930
You know, you're giving
your best is what allows us,

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:10.140
which has kept this program
strong for 25 years.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:14.220
You know in this program
you've demonstrated dedication,

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:16.050
persistence and resilience.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.040
You know, science is hard,

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:19.195
it has a lot of setbacks

00:07:19.195 --> 00:07:23.443
but your ability to overcome
these challenges, bad data,

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:27.120
just, you know, hard to long days,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.060
you know, it's what you've
demonstrated in the labs

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:35.160
that again has made this
program so successful.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.090
And, you know, nothing makes me happier

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:41.503
than to see our students, you know,

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:45.010
thrive through this program

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:49.530
and to have good rewards after graduation,

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.970
good careers after graduation

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:54.750
based on the experiences that
you've gained through STARS.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:57.590
And so dean I'm grateful
for this opportunity

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:59.330
to thank you all,

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:01.730
and I wanna just close by saying

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:04.130
that it's absolutely a top goal for me

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:05.936
to expand this program

00:08:05.936 --> 00:08:10.150
so that every student
who wants to participate

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:11.860
can benefit from it.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:16.220
And so I really hope even as
soon as the 30th anniversary

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.380
and ideally even sooner,

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:21.280
I or my successor will be able to say

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.223
that this program is fully funded.

00:08:24.958 --> 00:08:28.140
So, but today I look forward
to celebrating this milestone

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:28.973
with you all,

00:08:28.973 --> 00:08:31.090
and I thank you all for being here.

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:31.923
Have a good day.

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:34.430
- Thank you so much Marvin Chun,

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:38.310
and thank you for your
continued support for Yale STEM.

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:41.000
Our next speaker is Kim Goff-Crews,

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:45.470
the secretary and vice
president for university life.

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:46.513
Secretary Crews.

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:49.189
- Thank you Dr. Chang.

00:08:49.189 --> 00:08:52.570
First of all, I am so excited to be here,

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:55.600
I'm honored to be here
to celebrate with you

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:58.040
this milestone in STARS history.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:08:59.990
I, as people mentioned was there

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:01.453
at the very, very beginning,

00:09:02.787 --> 00:09:06.050
and I'm surprised and grateful

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:07.740
that it has lasted as long as it has,

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.140
I wasn't sure that it would.

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:13.805
Professor Zilm and I were
part of a small team of...

00:09:13.805 --> 00:09:17.483
And Dr. Nelson also, a small
team of faculty and staff

00:09:17.483 --> 00:09:19.990
that were looking to grow a program

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:21.900
particularly for students
of color and women

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:26.760
that would ensure that they
could persist in the sciences.

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:29.960
And it's interesting because
it's succeeded a program

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:31.513
that was called AMPS.

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:34.270
It was called the
Academic Mentoring Program

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:35.764
in the Sciences.

00:09:35.764 --> 00:09:37.550
That wasn't really working as well.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:39.680
So we took a year off
to actually figure out

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:40.730
what we wanted to do.

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:42.390
And most people don't know this,

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:44.580
that before STARS there was nothing,

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:45.510
it was a year of nothing,

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:48.160
and then we actually went
on a little bit of a tour

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:51.850
and doing some research around the country

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:54.570
to figure out what we
might do differently.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:59.070
And with that wonderful team
of Ken Nelson, back to Zilm,

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:01.783
Iona Black came in just at the right time,

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.210
and Kayla's came in a little later.

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:09.270
We began to, actually the faculty
began to develop a program

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.860
that they thought was really
worthy of the students,

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:13.840
but they wanted to provide service for.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.570
They had four but main themes

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:17.560
that they were really focusing on

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:19.440
as they were building the program.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.100
One is, we were all focused
on following the students,

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:24.400
particularly that initial student group,

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:25.450
what their needs were,

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:28.397
and so STARS I was the
first iteration of STARS,

00:10:28.397 --> 00:10:29.230
then STARS Summer,

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:31.660
because the students were
gonna go into summer program,

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:33.550
then STARS II because then
the students were becoming,

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.640
you know, becoming sophomores and juniors

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:37.280
so we followed the students.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.790
We were very focused on
focusing on the whole student

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:42.660
and not just their academic work.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.410
And we were very focused on
having very high expectations

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:48.730
but also creating community
and not competition.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:50.190
And so you'll see those things, I think,

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:54.730
have really gone through
STARS for the last 25 years

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:56.610
with this wonderful group
of faculty and staff

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:59.930
who have nurtured it
over the years as well.

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:01.470
I am grateful to those initial students

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:05.760
who allowed us to work
so closely with them,

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.940
and to Kayla Suntory,

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:11.020
particularly to Dr. Black as
well, who passed last summer

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:12.980
but who was such a guiding light

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:15.783
as a wonderful mentor for
the students over the years.

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:18.513
I know she, wherever she
is, is pleased to see

00:11:18.513 --> 00:11:20.210
that this program is thriving

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:23.060
to see the work that her students
she worked with are doing,

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:25.980
and to see that all of
you have continued to do

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:28.200
and participate in a
program that is thriving.

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:29.390
So again, thank you.

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:31.210
Congratulations to all STARS,

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:33.740
to the administrative have followed,

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:36.650
and it's the wonderful
work that we see coming out

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:38.420
of the program itself.

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:39.443
Congratulations.

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:43.670
- Thank you very much
Secretary Goff-Crews,

00:11:43.670 --> 00:11:46.410
and thank you for founding STARS.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:49.150
Our next speaker is Kurt Zilm,

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:53.240
the William K. Lanman Jr.
Professor of Chemistry and Chair

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:55.460
of the Department of Chemistry at Yale.

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:56.790
Professor Zilm.

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:57.623
- Thank you Sandy.

00:11:57.623 --> 00:11:59.340
It's a real pleasure to be here.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:04.340
I, you know, it's hard
to believe 25 years,

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:07.150
I would have never
thought starting with AMPS

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:10.190
and I've got to mention
one name I haven't heard

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:14.570
is Bob Weimann, Professor of NCDB,

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:17.903
who I think really was the founder.

00:12:17.903 --> 00:12:20.390
I've been given credit
for a lot of things,

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:21.883
about why AMPS started,

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:26.490
especially when this
became an HHMI Program

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:28.133
and then we've ever since,

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:31.330
you know, through many rounds of funding,

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:35.093
you know, when this was innovative,

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:39.180
across the country we were copied,

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.740
it was the most sincere form of flattery

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:45.620
by other universities copying
what was done in STARS.

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:49.810
But after about 15 years
or so, 10 or 15 years,

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:50.643
funding agencies started saying,

00:12:50.643 --> 00:12:52.530
"Well, this isn't innovative anymore.

00:12:52.530 --> 00:12:54.900
This is just something you should do.

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:57.170
We don't need to provide
seed funding anymore."

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:01.120
And so it's been my...

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:05.380
My goal has been to push the institution

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:08.883
to find a way to endow this
program into fully funded.

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:14.770
It's so fulfilling to hear first from,

00:13:16.916 --> 00:13:19.840
at the time was Dean
Holloway and now Dean Chang,

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:22.730
and to hear in the Yale science priorities

00:13:22.730 --> 00:13:24.740
that this is an institutional priority

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:27.908
and it's not a question of if but when,

00:13:27.908 --> 00:13:32.908
and I hope you're all able
to participate and help us,

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:38.160
you know, in this goal of making
certain this program is fun

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:39.620
and it can be expanded

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:42.530
for future generations of Yale students.

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:44.083
I wish you were all here.

00:13:45.586 --> 00:13:49.600
There's so much has changed
since you were on campus

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:50.990
many of you.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:54.240
There has been an enormous
amount of building

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:55.073
that's going on.

00:13:55.073 --> 00:13:56.690
I'm speaking to you

00:13:56.690 --> 00:13:59.670
with the background
for my renovated office

00:13:59.670 --> 00:14:00.830
in Sterling Chemistry

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:03.590
and I'm very proud of my labs

00:14:03.590 --> 00:14:06.010
that are in Sterling Chemistry,

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:08.800
but the spaces that you were
probably most familiar with,

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:13.030
you know, where maybe you
took a class in SCL 160

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:16.970
which is about half
again, bigger than it was

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:19.719
with the demonstration hoods,

00:14:19.719 --> 00:14:24.719
the old teaching labs that
you had with the wooden sashes

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:31.560
have been replaced by an
amazing new teaching facility

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.910
where every student in organic chemistry

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:37.735
gets their own hood to use.

00:14:37.735 --> 00:14:40.390
It's just an amazing really facility

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:44.120
that just showcases
Yale's commitment to STEM.

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:46.690
There's a new research building.

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:47.523
That's actually Sterling.

00:14:47.523 --> 00:14:50.080
There's a new research
building for chemistry

00:14:51.170 --> 00:14:55.090
and there is now a new biology building

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:57.380
that's replaced Gibbs,

00:14:57.380 --> 00:14:58.320
which is amazing.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:01.570
So it, you know, I really
wish we were on campus.

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:03.250
I hope we have an on-campus one

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:07.630
so you'd all get a chance
to see the facilities

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:10.460
that STARS students are
able to work in now.

00:15:10.460 --> 00:15:14.260
And I'm very excited to hear about,

00:15:14.260 --> 00:15:15.540
in the breakout sessions where...

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:19.972
What you've all been up to and to catch up

00:15:19.972 --> 00:15:22.360
and hopefully be able to engage you

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:25.660
to help feature first
STARS students and Yale

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:27.940
as we try to fully fund this program.

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:28.773
Thank you,

00:15:28.773 --> 00:15:29.606
and welcome.

00:15:30.460 --> 00:15:32.660
- Thank you very much Professor Zilm.

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:37.660
Our first STARS alumni
speaker is Carissa Sanchez.

00:15:37.823 --> 00:15:42.193
The facility administrator
at DaVita Dialysis Clinic.

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:43.943
Carissa.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:49.520
- Hi, can everyone hear me all right?

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:50.950
All right. Sounds good.

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:53.600
Thank you so much for having
me with you guys this morning.

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:55.130
I'm very excited to be here.

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:57.940
I was a mechanical engineering major

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:01.460
and graduated from Yale in 2015,

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:05.510
and then went on to really to
do actual engineering work.

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:06.710
So I worked with Alcoa.

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:09.720
We were doing a lot of
aerospace manufacturing

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:11.690
for jet engine components,

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:14.520
and got a lot of my exposure there.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:17.480
I came in through the
STARS Summer Program,

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:21.510
and maybe like a lot of other, you know,

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:23.800
first-generation URM students,

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:26.740
I honestly had no idea
what I wanted to study

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:29.380
and yeah, very much joined STARS

00:16:29.380 --> 00:16:30.950
and very luckily matched

00:16:30.950 --> 00:16:34.030
with the Schroeder's material science

00:16:34.030 --> 00:16:35.840
and mechanical engineering lab,

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:38.370
and was really grateful
for the mentorship,

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:40.823
especially of Kyla who I see them today,

00:16:40.823 --> 00:16:43.890
and yeah, I was able to find out

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:45.690
that I really enjoyed the research.

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:47.940
I also kind of serendipitously found

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:49.710
that there was a very strong connection

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:51.900
with these materials
that we were developing

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:55.360
that could be used for
healthcare applications.

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:58.610
And I ended up staying
with that same lab I found

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:01.630
during my start summer for
all four years of undergrad,

00:17:01.630 --> 00:17:05.330
and was able to help a PhD student

00:17:05.330 --> 00:17:07.900
develop these diabetes
blood glucose sensors

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:09.890
out of the materials we were developing,

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:13.010
and that kind of spurred the
rest of my career honestly

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:15.040
because I had this light bulb moment

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:16.480
where I realized that, you know,

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:18.650
I'm from a ton lots of nation

00:17:18.650 --> 00:17:21.320
and my tribe has the
highest rate of diabetes

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:24.380
for any people group
from across the world.

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:27.149
So over 50% of the TRIPOD diabetes,

00:17:27.149 --> 00:17:29.140
five or 10 or 25 years old,

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:32.550
and just STARS, you know, curriculum

00:17:32.550 --> 00:17:33.726
and being able to go through that

00:17:33.726 --> 00:17:35.500
just really lit a fire underneath

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:37.960
to be able to have a
good career within STEM

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:39.760
but also to do something
good for my people

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:41.323
in underrepresented community.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:45.680
So, you know, going through
my actual engineering job,

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:47.050
it was fun, I was learning a lot.

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:49.800
I also wanted to do more

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:51.600
and, you know, get back to doing something

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:54.130
that would impact my people.

00:17:54.130 --> 00:17:56.050
So I went to business school,

00:17:56.050 --> 00:17:59.070
I'm a little off the
be impact for typical,

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:01.080
you know, STEM engineering student,

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:03.500
and wound up, you know,

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:05.510
kind of serendipitously finding DaVita

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:07.520
who provides a lot of dialysis care,

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:09.310
and they're also on the innovation front

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:12.070
of how do we address things like diabetes

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:14.810
and just general chronic kidney disease

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:16.540
from a big healthcare scale

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:19.050
and not just what a
doctor or a nurse is doing

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:21.360
every single day with the patients.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:26.040
So I joined the DaVita
Program after my MBA,

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:28.180
after spending a summer with CCG

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:31.260
and kind of getting additional
health care contacts there.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:34.070
And yeah, it's been a whirlwind.

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:37.130
I graduated from business
school in a little bit

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:39.330
about a year ago, so May 2020.

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:44.320
And it's been a fascinating
time to be in a dialysis clinic

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:47.200
during COVID when we were short staffed

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:48.290
or when things were going crazy,

00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:50.170
I was the person who was, you know,

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:51.270
running out to the parking lot

00:18:51.270 --> 00:18:53.140
and giving patients COVID tests.

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:55.190
So got a lot of good exposure there.

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:58.130
And it's been really heartwarming
to see that, you know,

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:02.900
the path from, you know,
a rural public school

00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:04.913
to Yale through STARS,

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:08.580
you know, through a
business school program

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:11.100
has led me to be able
to have a strong impact

00:19:12.070 --> 00:19:14.290
where, you know, I have a Navajo patient

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:16.700
who can't figure out how
to apply for Medicare,

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:17.550
I can help with that.

00:19:17.550 --> 00:19:20.270
And it's very rewarding
to be able to do that.

00:19:20.270 --> 00:19:22.760
So I want to encourage,

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:26.920
you know, the whole class
here and more recent alumni

00:19:26.920 --> 00:19:29.340
that your path may be winding.

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:32.300
I know we come into Yale thinking
like I'm gonna be a doctor

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:33.670
I'm gonna be a psychologist,

00:19:33.670 --> 00:19:35.490
I'm gonna be an engineer,

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:37.880
you know, the world throws
different things your way.

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:40.683
So be okay with that. It's
not the end of the world

00:19:40.683 --> 00:19:44.710
if plan A doesn't go exactly
how it was supposed to.

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:45.821
Certainly for me I had no idea

00:19:45.821 --> 00:19:48.900
that I would wind up in kidney care,

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:50.970
working directly with
native American populations,

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:52.950
which is, you know, very much the dream,

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:55.110
but it wasn't expected.

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:57.380
I would also like to encourage you guys

00:19:57.380 --> 00:19:59.100
to just take up space.

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:02.280
I think during my time at Yale,

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:04.020
I very much just like put my head down,

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:06.962
I think kind of on
stereotypical front minorities

00:20:06.962 --> 00:20:10.180
are trained to be quiet,

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:11.550
just like get through it and do well,

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:13.213
but not raise the rockets.

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:18.510
I think so much of my success was linked

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:21.840
to having close relationships
with the professors

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:23.750
and the PhD students and different mentors

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:25.890
who were really invested in our success,

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:29.070
and of course said like "You're
welcome to pop by anytime,"

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:32.050
but what that means is you
will be so much better off

00:20:32.050 --> 00:20:34.250
and have a much easier go of this

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:36.480
if you take up that office hour space

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:39.550
if you go have one-on-one
and build this relationship.

00:20:39.550 --> 00:20:43.770
So, you know, all of us here
speaking on the panel today

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:46.740
are very invested in
current student success,

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:48.720
so please reach out to any of us.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:49.790
I'm very open to that,

00:20:49.790 --> 00:20:51.820
very excited to pave the way

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:54.900
for you guys to do even
even better than we've done.

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:56.953
So, thanks for having me here today.

00:20:59.190 --> 00:21:02.780
- Thank you very much Carissa
for those essential advice.

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:05.170
Our next speaker is Courtney Williams,

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:07.240
Senior Staff Scientist,

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:09.720
Oncology and Angiogenesis Department

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:11.500
at Regeneron Pharmaceuticals.

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:12.333
Courtney.

00:21:13.190 --> 00:21:14.660
- Thanks so much Dean Chang.

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:18.506
I'm hoping you all can see my slides.

00:21:18.506 --> 00:21:20.410
My name's Courtney Williams.

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:23.540
I am class of O1.

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:26.590
This is my 20th reunion year,

00:21:26.590 --> 00:21:29.690
and I am glad Carissa went right before me

00:21:29.690 --> 00:21:32.270
because I think it is
important to remember

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:34.270
that your path can be a winding one

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:36.330
even though mine was not.

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:38.670
So I did go kind of straight shot

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:43.670
from undergrad to grad
school to a postdoc to a job.

00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:46.530
And some of that was kind of financial

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:49.420
not wanting to have my
loans come out of deferment

00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:50.360
and things like that,

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:54.460
but I think as the first
two speakers were examples

00:21:54.460 --> 00:21:56.080
that the paths can differ

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:59.100
and you can still get
to where you wanna go.

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:02.210
So that's some old pictures
here for everybody.

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:07.210
So, I was at Yale from...

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:08.650
I'm sorry, I'm just gonna have a point

00:22:08.650 --> 00:22:09.630
or second point people out.

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:12.220
From 1997 to 2001,

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:16.210
I majored in MBMB and I
was able to participate

00:22:16.210 --> 00:22:19.110
in STARS I, STARS Summer, STARS II,

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:22.080
all of that thanks to Dr. Iona black,

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:24.890
who you heard mentioned
earlier, who's right here,

00:22:24.890 --> 00:22:26.960
who saw me in her chemistry class

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:30.380
and just for whatever reason decided to...

00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:32.570
Like I was gonna be in her program

00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:35.930
and she was gonna make
sure that that happened.

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:38.970
And it allowed me to get into a lab.

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:41.350
I worked with Dr. Joann Sweasy

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:45.750
who was part of the larger
graduate school faculty

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:49.360
but her lab was in the Department
of Therapeutic Radiology

00:22:49.360 --> 00:22:50.770
at the Yale School of Medicine.

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:55.720
She since moved her lab
recently to Arizona.

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:59.780
But there I worked on
Polymerase-Beta interacting proteins

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:04.630
during DNA mismatch repair
in mammalian miosis.

00:23:04.630 --> 00:23:06.590
And just to give you an idea

00:23:06.590 --> 00:23:09.640
these are some of my friends from STARS.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:12.340
Now a doctor, now a physician's assistant,

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:14.400
now doing research,

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:17.460
Jessica is in your program.

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:19.883
That's, she and I were
there at the same time.

00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:23.830
Some of you might remember Dean George.

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:28.830
So lots of folks went
on to successful careers

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:33.210
in science and law and
lots of other things.

00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:34.590
But when I finished up at Yale,

00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:36.550
I considered staying there for grad school

00:23:36.550 --> 00:23:39.720
but felt like I kind of
needed to leave the nest.

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:43.090
And so I went to Princeton

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:46.240
where I was in the Department
of Molecular Biology

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:49.960
and it was a relatively
small program at the time.

00:23:49.960 --> 00:23:53.190
It's since grown tremendously
since I've been there.

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:56.350
I was in the lab of
Dr. Jean Schwartzbauer,

00:23:56.350 --> 00:23:59.080
and I've just put some
of my publications here

00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:04.080
just so students can get
an idea of the kind of work

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:06.680
that I did in these various labs.

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:09.480
But my project was
looking at breast cancer

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:13.070
and the role of the extracellular
matrix protein fibronectin

00:24:13.070 --> 00:24:16.360
in breast cancer
initiation and progression.

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:19.490
And I did use the 3D culture system

00:24:19.490 --> 00:24:24.002
that was pretty cumbersome
and not the best way

00:24:24.002 --> 00:24:28.560
to look at these problems and
ask these kinds of questions

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:30.580
about the role of matrix and stiffness

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:33.120
in breast cancer progression.

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:36.720
And at a conference that I went to,

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:41.350
senior scientists suggested
to think of your career

00:24:42.370 --> 00:24:43.730
like a tool box.

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:46.050
And what are the questions you wanna ask

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:48.690
and what tools do you
need in that toolbox,

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:51.200
and then pick your next steps

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:53.610
based on how to get those tools.

00:24:53.610 --> 00:24:55.560
And so I took that advice to heart,

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:58.530
I realized that I knew
a lot about the biology

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:00.560
but I didn't know a lot
about the engineering

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:03.090
that it would take to design systems

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:04.180
that I really needed

00:25:04.180 --> 00:25:07.270
to ask the kind of questions
that I wanted to ask.

00:25:07.270 --> 00:25:08.800
So from there,

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:11.730
I went to MIT for my post-doc

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:13.850
in the lab of Dr. Linda Griffith

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:16.170
in the Department of
Biological Engineering.

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:17.570
And some people do know her,

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:19.430
she's a MacArthur genius grant winner

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:23.470
but if you've ever seen the
picture of the nude mouse

00:25:23.470 --> 00:25:26.130
with a human ear growing on its back,

00:25:26.130 --> 00:25:30.530
that was her post-doctoral
research that produced that.

00:25:30.530 --> 00:25:32.870
And so in her lab there at MIT

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:37.420
I basically was able to
up my engineering game

00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:39.790
and work on designing culture systems

00:25:39.790 --> 00:25:43.720
to help maintain the function
of primary hepatocytes.

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:45.470
Which is important for drug testing

00:25:45.470 --> 00:25:49.200
because if these cells don't
maintain their differentiation

00:25:49.200 --> 00:25:52.270
then you can't look at
toxicity because you don't know

00:25:52.270 --> 00:25:55.260
if drugs are being properly metabolized.

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:59.430
But so I worked with a
team of material scientists

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:04.030
and other engineers to come
up with two different systems

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:06.470
for culturing these primary hepatocytes

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:08.860
to get them to maintain that function.

00:26:08.860 --> 00:26:13.440
And when I went looking for a job,

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:16.070
I had previously seen information

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:17.720
about Regeneron Pharmaceuticals

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:19.280
and it was near where I grew up.

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:22.080
I grew up in the Hudson
Valley in New York State

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:26.160
and it turns out my cousin's

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:30.650
wife's best friend from high
school worked at Regeneron.

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:32.610
And I found that out through LinkedIn

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:37.430
and that connection I
had, I talked with her,

00:26:37.430 --> 00:26:38.810
my cousin put me in touch

00:26:38.810 --> 00:26:43.810
and she sent my CV on to the
HR department at Regeneron,

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:46.900
and it turned out that they
were looking for someone

00:26:46.900 --> 00:26:50.100
to design in vitro cell culture systems

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:53.500
to compliment their in vivo tumor work.

00:26:53.500 --> 00:26:56.650
And basically I was kind of
the perfect candidate for that

00:26:56.650 --> 00:26:59.890
because I came from a background of,

00:26:59.890 --> 00:27:01.430
instead of year one,

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:06.430
culture system is the solution
for all engineering needs.

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:09.167
I approached it from a much more like

00:27:09.167 --> 00:27:11.390
"What signals do you need?

00:27:11.390 --> 00:27:14.980
This is how we can ask those questions."

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:17.080
And so I've been there for 10 years,

00:27:17.080 --> 00:27:19.130
and I started as a scientist

00:27:19.130 --> 00:27:21.790
and I've studied a bunch
of different things

00:27:21.790 --> 00:27:23.700
as well as doing a lot of STEM outreach,

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:25.477
these are some of my publications here,

00:27:25.477 --> 00:27:27.727
and I can talk more about
that in the breakout group

00:27:27.727 --> 00:27:30.010
'cause I don't wanna go over time,

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:32.630
and I definitely want time for this slide.

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:36.200
So this is the T-shirt that
I still have with our logo

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:40.010
from the STARS Summer in 1999.

00:27:40.010 --> 00:27:41.380
That is me,

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:44.093
and that is my friend
Lauren, who's now a doctor,

00:27:44.930 --> 00:27:47.630
and some more pictures
we've got, you know,

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:49.740
a Marine biologist,

00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:51.020
we've got some lawyers,

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:55.460
there's Dr. Black,
researcher, wealth capital.

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:57.200
I looked up some folks last night,

00:27:57.200 --> 00:27:59.520
wealth capital person,

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:02.970
doctor at Memorial Sloan Kettering.

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:06.810
So a lot of people went on to
do a lot of successful stuff.

00:28:06.810 --> 00:28:09.283
We've got people in education,

00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:11.880
plenty of doctors.

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:15.490
I still run into STARS people
working in the various areas

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:17.580
of the life sciences,

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:19.763
but it's definitely been,

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:25.130
STARS was instrumental into
helping me stay in science

00:28:25.130 --> 00:28:26.930
and be successful in science.

00:28:26.930 --> 00:28:29.810
And I really owe that all to Dr. Black,

00:28:29.810 --> 00:28:34.240
who was leading STARS when
I was an undergraduate

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:36.640
and kind of saw something in me,

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:41.100
and decided to really despite
my resistance to joining STARS

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:44.740
really decided to make
sure I got in the program,

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:49.010
and she really was a champion
for me as an undergraduate

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:52.730
and the program that
STARS was with Dr. Nelson

00:28:52.730 --> 00:28:56.250
was really instrumental into
my success as an undergrad

00:28:56.250 --> 00:29:00.050
and leaving me very well
prepared for graduate school.

00:29:00.050 --> 00:29:02.860
So with that, I am
finished a little over time

00:29:02.860 --> 00:29:06.583
but I look forward to talking
to students in the breakout,

00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:08.710
in the breakout groups.

00:29:08.710 --> 00:29:10.333
So, thanks so much.

00:29:11.810 --> 00:29:13.950
- Thank you very much Courtney.

00:29:13.950 --> 00:29:16.280
Our next speaker is Thomas Howard,

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:20.640
a current medical student at
Harvard MIT MD/PhD program.

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:21.473
Thomas.

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:24.880
- Thank you Sandy.

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:27.583
I hope everyone can see my slides here.

00:29:28.669 --> 00:29:32.350
I wanted to speak briefly today
about some of my experiences

00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:35.130
as a first-generation college student,

00:29:35.130 --> 00:29:38.635
and in particular how in medical
school it really felt like

00:29:38.635 --> 00:29:42.210
all of my classmates had
physicians for parents.

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:45.330
And now this wasn't quite true, of course,

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:49.010
but there definitely seemed
to be this wealth of knowledge

00:29:49.010 --> 00:29:50.980
that many of my classmates had,

00:29:50.980 --> 00:29:53.060
that wasn't taught in our classrooms

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:55.540
and weren't in any of our books.

00:29:55.540 --> 00:29:58.020
And I've one of the many examples of this

00:29:58.020 --> 00:30:03.020
on my very first rotation
coming back from my PhD years

00:30:03.110 --> 00:30:04.160
was surgery,

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:07.870
and the intern handed me
a copy of the patient list

00:30:07.870 --> 00:30:10.684
that might've looked something like this,

00:30:10.684 --> 00:30:14.720
and it's mostly a list of
tasks that need to get done,

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:17.070
and as the medical student,
you're trying to be helpful,

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:20.850
you're trying to get some of
these done for the residents.

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:23.280
And as you can see, so much of medicine

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:25.740
has all of this shorthand and acronyms

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:27.810
to try to figure out what's going on

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:29.100
with each of these patients,

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:32.830
and fortunately I knew many of this.

00:30:32.830 --> 00:30:35.210
But there was one acronym
that was popping up

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:37.120
all over the place, NTD

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:39.690
and I had no idea what that meant.

00:30:39.690 --> 00:30:42.000
I was racking my brain
to try to figure out

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:46.200
what test or antibiotic or
whatever this might mean

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:47.690
and when I failed at that,

00:30:47.690 --> 00:30:49.700
I did whatever every
did medical student does

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:52.280
which is consult Dr. Google.

00:30:52.280 --> 00:30:54.360
But every place that I looked,

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:58.130
the only thing I saw for
NTD was a Neural Tube Defect

00:30:58.130 --> 00:31:01.210
which is something that can
happen in the developing fetus

00:31:01.210 --> 00:31:04.209
and certainly was not
the medical abbreviation

00:31:04.209 --> 00:31:06.660
that I was looking for.

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:09.660
So I sheepishly asked the intern,

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:12.850
I said I'm so sorry if I should know this,

00:31:12.850 --> 00:31:15.540
but could you please
tell me what NTD means.

00:31:15.540 --> 00:31:19.520
And then that's when she said
it just means nothing to do.

00:31:19.520 --> 00:31:22.330
It wasn't a medical abbreviation at all,

00:31:22.330 --> 00:31:25.833
it was just a shorthand
on that list of tasks.

00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:28.050
Now, when I think back

00:31:28.050 --> 00:31:31.990
at what has been 13 years
of higher education,

00:31:31.990 --> 00:31:35.600
I really appreciate the
fact that I had a chance

00:31:35.600 --> 00:31:37.750
as part of the STARS Summer program,

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:40.370
to learn many of these things.

00:31:40.370 --> 00:31:44.110
And so that's both the
programming that recognizes

00:31:44.110 --> 00:31:46.677
that not all of us come in
with the same background

00:31:46.677 --> 00:31:48.660
and the same information,

00:31:48.660 --> 00:31:51.680
but then more importantly
connecting us with mentors

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:55.210
who can try to help us
navigate those waters.

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:59.300
At that point, I had no idea
what graduate school was

00:31:59.300 --> 00:32:02.660
or what one might do with a PhD

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:05.280
or what being a PI might be like.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:07.310
And I'm so thankful for Steve and Maria

00:32:07.310 --> 00:32:09.450
who welcomed me into their lab

00:32:09.450 --> 00:32:11.460
and taught me all of these things,

00:32:11.460 --> 00:32:12.870
and then put me on the road

00:32:12.870 --> 00:32:16.193
to applying for MD/PhD
programs after Yale.

00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:18.600
I was also very fortunate

00:32:18.600 --> 00:32:21.690
to have great PhD mentors at Harvard,

00:32:21.690 --> 00:32:24.320
and particularly people who
could model what it was like

00:32:24.320 --> 00:32:27.923
to care for patients as
well as to run a lap.

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:31.510
And now I think many of you guys

00:32:31.510 --> 00:32:33.800
are already experiencing that.

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:35.340
As soon as you want information

00:32:35.340 --> 00:32:38.310
you can start passing
that on to others as well.

00:32:38.310 --> 00:32:40.550
And so one of the things
that I'm most proud of

00:32:40.550 --> 00:32:43.810
of my time in those
labs is that I recruited

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:46.830
and was able to mentor
an undergrad, Melinda

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:48.890
and a research technician, Taylor,

00:32:48.890 --> 00:32:52.860
who were both phenomenal
and so helpful for our work.

00:32:52.860 --> 00:32:55.540
And we had all of these people contribute

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:59.420
to many of them who were post-docs
or professors themselves,

00:32:59.420 --> 00:33:00.710
and it was Melinda and Taylor

00:33:00.710 --> 00:33:03.790
who we were able to
give the most credit for

00:33:03.790 --> 00:33:06.090
as part of this team effort.

00:33:06.090 --> 00:33:07.200
And what I'm most proud of

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:11.210
is we were also able to help
them even in just little ways

00:33:11.210 --> 00:33:13.570
to get them into medical school themselves

00:33:13.570 --> 00:33:15.160
and they're gonna move
on with their dreams

00:33:15.160 --> 00:33:17.053
to become doctors as well.

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:19.920
And lastly, I just wanted to mention

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:21.470
that the greatest gift I got from Yale

00:33:21.470 --> 00:33:24.550
was meeting my now wife, Tara.

00:33:24.550 --> 00:33:26.500
We got annexed from our colleges,

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:28.580
junior year to Arnold hall.

00:33:28.580 --> 00:33:30.850
I'm not sure if that's
something that still happens

00:33:30.850 --> 00:33:33.520
but if that's an experience you're having,

00:33:33.520 --> 00:33:36.470
there might be some unexpected
positives that come from it.

00:33:37.360 --> 00:33:38.820
So thank you all for listening.

00:33:38.820 --> 00:33:40.310
I'm so excited that we'll be able to talk

00:33:40.310 --> 00:33:41.710
with some of you later on,

00:33:41.710 --> 00:33:45.460
and I'm very happy to reach out by email

00:33:45.460 --> 00:33:48.110
or anything that I can be
helpful with in the future.

00:33:49.490 --> 00:33:51.740
- Thank you very much Thomas.

00:33:51.740 --> 00:33:54.650
Our next speaker is Aida Behmard

00:33:54.650 --> 00:33:57.729
who's a current graduate
student and NSF fellow.

00:33:57.729 --> 00:33:59.230
Aida.

00:33:59.230 --> 00:34:00.063
- Yeah, sure.

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:02.520
So right, my name is Aida Behmard.

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:06.740
I graduated from Yale in
2015 with a BS in Physics,

00:34:06.740 --> 00:34:08.520
and I was a STARS II student

00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:10.340
and I'm currently a grad student

00:34:10.340 --> 00:34:12.535
in Planetary Science at Caltech.

00:34:12.535 --> 00:34:15.600
So my current research
is focused on exoplanets

00:34:15.600 --> 00:34:17.980
which is basically exactly
what they sound like.

00:34:17.980 --> 00:34:21.110
They're planets that exist
outside the solar system,

00:34:21.110 --> 00:34:23.460
orbiting stars other than the sun.

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:26.530
So these days I'm working on
projects to try and tease out

00:34:26.530 --> 00:34:29.740
possible correlations between
the chemical compositions

00:34:29.740 --> 00:34:32.090
of the stars that host these exoplanets

00:34:32.090 --> 00:34:34.260
and the types of the planets themselves.

00:34:34.260 --> 00:34:36.290
So one example of this could be,

00:34:36.290 --> 00:34:40.160
does a star rich in rocky
elements like iron or magnesium

00:34:40.160 --> 00:34:43.640
tend to host planetary systems
of a certain type like say,

00:34:43.640 --> 00:34:45.240
maybe close in gas giants

00:34:45.240 --> 00:34:48.190
or rather smaller multi planet systems.

00:34:48.190 --> 00:34:50.350
And figuring out these
sorts of correlations

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:53.320
can tell us more about the
chemical histories of planets

00:34:53.320 --> 00:34:56.180
outside our solar system
which I find super fascinating

00:34:56.180 --> 00:34:58.980
because I think of other
planets as not just things

00:34:58.980 --> 00:35:00.550
but other places,

00:35:00.550 --> 00:35:02.130
places that we can learn more about

00:35:02.130 --> 00:35:03.790
and maybe travel to one day,

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:06.290
and maybe amenable to hosting life.

00:35:06.290 --> 00:35:08.090
So I'm super grateful for the start

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:09.800
I got in science research at Yale,

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:13.060
a large part of which was
participation in the STARS program

00:35:13.060 --> 00:35:14.970
which allowed me to conduct research

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:16.020
in the Astro departments,

00:35:16.020 --> 00:35:19.020
and also gave me access to
a community of scientists

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:20.540
who were working on really cool research

00:35:20.540 --> 00:35:21.940
that was very different from my own

00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:24.020
which is always nice to hear about.

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:26.640
And the mentorship I
received as a STARS student

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:28.530
was also super helpful for me.

00:35:28.530 --> 00:35:31.300
I was able to build close
relationships with postdocs

00:35:31.300 --> 00:35:32.940
and other senior scientists at Yale,

00:35:32.940 --> 00:35:35.800
who also were women of color
and who were honest with me

00:35:35.800 --> 00:35:38.360
about challenges that I
might face in grad school,

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:41.690
and encouraged me that I
would be able to do it anyway.

00:35:41.690 --> 00:35:43.507
So these days when I'm
not working on science

00:35:43.507 --> 00:35:46.040
I spend a lot of time on diversity, equity

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:48.330
and inclusion initiatives at Caltech.

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:50.350
I currently serve as the diversity chair

00:35:50.350 --> 00:35:51.670
on the grad student council

00:35:51.670 --> 00:35:53.440
and we do everything
that we can to support

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:57.160
and advocate for Caltech grads
with marginalized identities.

00:35:57.160 --> 00:36:00.170
I'm also really passionate
about science education outreach

00:36:00.170 --> 00:36:02.770
and I've been going into
Pasadena Elementary Schools

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:04.550
as part of a grad student team

00:36:04.550 --> 00:36:06.290
to teach bi-weekly science classes

00:36:06.290 --> 00:36:08.440
for the better part of
the last four years.

00:36:09.460 --> 00:36:11.660
I honestly didn't think I
would spend so much time

00:36:11.660 --> 00:36:14.410
on outreach and DI initiatives
when I got to Caltech,

00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:16.350
but it turns out that Caltech,

00:36:16.350 --> 00:36:19.410
while an exceptional institute
for science and research

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:20.880
is not the most diverse place,

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:22.060
which I think on some level

00:36:22.060 --> 00:36:24.400
can be said about any scientific community

00:36:24.400 --> 00:36:27.290
especially once you climb
past the undergrad level.

00:36:27.290 --> 00:36:28.670
So when I came and saw how it was

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:31.680
I really wanted to do everything to help

00:36:31.680 --> 00:36:32.940
that was in my power.

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:35.170
So if you're interested in
talking to me about any of that

00:36:35.170 --> 00:36:36.870
or my science or anything at all

00:36:36.870 --> 00:36:38.623
I'd be super happy to do so later.

00:36:40.450 --> 00:36:42.090
- Thank you very much Aida.

00:36:42.090 --> 00:36:44.397
Our next speaker is Ben Bartolome

00:36:44.397 --> 00:36:46.600
who is a current Data Scientist

00:36:46.600 --> 00:36:49.010
at Navy Federal Credit Union.

00:36:49.010 --> 00:36:49.843
Ben.

00:36:51.080 --> 00:36:53.173
- Hey, good morning everyone.

00:36:54.210 --> 00:36:55.530
Yeah, my name is Ben Bartolome,

00:36:55.530 --> 00:36:56.600
class of 2016.

00:36:58.030 --> 00:37:00.080
Majored in Chemical Engineering,

00:37:00.080 --> 00:37:04.790
and I'm now a data scientist
at a pretty big credit union.

00:37:04.790 --> 00:37:09.117
Before I start, I want to give
a shout out to my girl, Mary

00:37:11.460 --> 00:37:12.860
who's a current STARS I student,

00:37:12.860 --> 00:37:16.720
super awesome, really
incredible person and all the...

00:37:16.720 --> 00:37:18.860
And I really look forward to chatting

00:37:18.860 --> 00:37:23.703
with as many of you as
possible in the coming years.

00:37:25.180 --> 00:37:28.810
In terms of how STARS has
impacted me in my journey,

00:37:28.810 --> 00:37:31.940
I want to give a few
pretty material examples

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:34.893
but on perhaps like on
the softer skill side.

00:37:35.970 --> 00:37:38.020
The ability to clearly communicate

00:37:38.020 --> 00:37:43.020
as perhaps better evidence than
Courtney and Thomas's slides

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:46.780
is really, really important.

00:37:46.780 --> 00:37:51.515
And you'll see some abysmal
truly important things

00:37:51.515 --> 00:37:53.180
as you go about your life.

00:37:53.180 --> 00:37:54.970
And so I'm gonna give you an example.

00:37:54.970 --> 00:37:59.970
I just had this slide shown
to me earlier this week

00:38:00.540 --> 00:38:02.503
and at work about,

00:38:04.007 --> 00:38:06.830
they're about 200 slides,
this one stood up the most.

00:38:06.830 --> 00:38:11.120
And these were created
by Accenture consultants

00:38:11.120 --> 00:38:13.880
who get paid six figures.

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:15.590
And you don't have to know
what any of this means.

00:38:15.590 --> 00:38:16.910
I don't know what any of this means,

00:38:16.910 --> 00:38:18.520
but someone sat down,

00:38:18.520 --> 00:38:20.497
they built this slide and they thought,

00:38:20.497 --> 00:38:22.450
"Yeah, this is really the clearest way

00:38:22.450 --> 00:38:24.390
to communicate this information.

00:38:24.390 --> 00:38:25.670
This is abysmal.

00:38:25.670 --> 00:38:26.940
Who knows what's going on here?"

00:38:26.940 --> 00:38:29.110
And then ironically that
half year in the middle

00:38:29.110 --> 00:38:31.890
some circle thing that
says elicit and clarify.

00:38:31.890 --> 00:38:33.130
This is a joke.

00:38:33.130 --> 00:38:35.690
And so, you know,

00:38:35.690 --> 00:38:40.120
this would not be a professional
ano approved, I'm sure,

00:38:40.120 --> 00:38:42.830
but in terms of clear communication

00:38:42.830 --> 00:38:44.060
this is the way to not do it.

00:38:44.060 --> 00:38:47.413
STARS will help you
get on the right track.

00:38:48.331 --> 00:38:50.443
To start everybody off,

00:38:51.510 --> 00:38:54.393
well, in terms of other things,

00:38:55.420 --> 00:38:57.760
and thankfully there's a
breakout room on this later

00:38:57.760 --> 00:38:59.570
which is awesome,

00:38:59.570 --> 00:39:03.720
but STARS also taught me how
to be a really good mentee

00:39:03.720 --> 00:39:06.020
and also an improving mentor,

00:39:06.020 --> 00:39:09.030
which is to say that like as
a mentee and also as a mentor,

00:39:09.030 --> 00:39:10.627
but like,

00:39:10.627 --> 00:39:12.940
you know,

00:39:12.940 --> 00:39:14.500
throughout your journey with STARS

00:39:14.500 --> 00:39:17.140
you get the opportunity to
learn how to listen well,

00:39:17.140 --> 00:39:19.590
to give conversations
like the space to breathe,

00:39:19.590 --> 00:39:22.170
how to enter conversations
with like a humility

00:39:22.170 --> 00:39:23.770
and an open mind,

00:39:23.770 --> 00:39:26.180
asking thoughtful questions of the people

00:39:26.180 --> 00:39:27.820
that you're in dialogue with.

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:31.480
And then, you know, on either
side of the interaction,

00:39:31.480 --> 00:39:34.377
the ability to like
for consistent followup

00:39:34.377 --> 00:39:37.120
and you know, there's a type
of thing where, you know,

00:39:37.120 --> 00:39:39.450
your mentor or advisor

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:42.177
or whoever that you
might be reaching out to,

00:39:42.177 --> 00:39:44.240
you know, reaching out,

00:39:44.240 --> 00:39:46.090
it doesn't have to be like
a kind of continuous thing,

00:39:46.090 --> 00:39:47.200
it can be,

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:50.340
but even something as perhaps 'infrequent'

00:39:50.340 --> 00:39:54.970
as a six months to a
year that type of thing,

00:39:54.970 --> 00:39:56.383
but it's super valuable.

00:39:57.596 --> 00:40:01.680
And it also emphasizes, you know,

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:04.780
in terms of this like
mentor/mentee relationship

00:40:04.780 --> 00:40:06.730
that there's like so many people,

00:40:06.730 --> 00:40:09.643
so many alums that want that help.

00:40:10.520 --> 00:40:13.100
You know, I think right now in total

00:40:14.610 --> 00:40:18.870
in terms of the veal alums,
there's like 175,000 plus.

00:40:18.870 --> 00:40:20.740
The overwhelming majority of them

00:40:20.740 --> 00:40:23.633
do not get reached out to
and they would love to help.

00:40:25.230 --> 00:40:28.360
And be like this is the way

00:40:28.360 --> 00:40:29.672
in which you can differentiate yourself,

00:40:29.672 --> 00:40:32.430
there's a massive massive amount of wisdom

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:34.060
that's available out there, right?

00:40:34.060 --> 00:40:35.430
And if you just reach out,

00:40:35.430 --> 00:40:37.667
there's a really high
likelihood that they'll say,

00:40:37.667 --> 00:40:38.900
"Oh yeah, (indistinct)"

00:40:38.900 --> 00:40:40.900
and you can have access to that.

00:40:40.900 --> 00:40:43.883
A really great example of
that on my end is that,

00:40:44.850 --> 00:40:46.450
excuse me,

00:40:46.450 --> 00:40:49.920
I am looking to start a
company soon with some friends

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:53.830
and I needed some insight,
kind of on the legal side

00:40:53.830 --> 00:40:55.580
and in the financial sector.

00:40:55.580 --> 00:40:57.750
And so I reached out to this one alumni

00:40:57.750 --> 00:41:01.590
who was the formerly a commissioner

00:41:01.590 --> 00:41:04.530
of the security and exchange
commission under Obama,

00:41:04.530 --> 00:41:06.850
and I was like, "Hey, can I
get your insight on this?"

00:41:06.850 --> 00:41:10.930
And she gave all kinds of
really valuable insight,

00:41:10.930 --> 00:41:12.360
and it was just by my reaching out

00:41:12.360 --> 00:41:13.280
and she was happy to help,

00:41:13.280 --> 00:41:15.580
and now she kind of
continues to be an advisor

00:41:15.580 --> 00:41:17.420
to me in many ways.

00:41:17.420 --> 00:41:18.805
But you know, all I had to do was ask.

00:41:18.805 --> 00:41:22.240
And so yeah, this concept
of being, you know,

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:24.960
a good mentee and similarly
on the mentor side

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:26.900
where you can develop your soccer skills

00:41:26.900 --> 00:41:28.400
under ordinarily (indistinct).

00:41:31.270 --> 00:41:32.103
And so yeah.

00:41:32.103 --> 00:41:33.550
And then the last thing
I wanna shout about

00:41:33.550 --> 00:41:37.320
and this is on a more personal
level, similar to Thomas,

00:41:37.320 --> 00:41:42.320
is that I want to talk about my...

00:41:44.650 --> 00:41:46.650
What else? Something
else that STARS provided

00:41:46.650 --> 00:41:49.460
was an avenue to meet my current wife.

00:41:49.460 --> 00:41:54.440
I met my wife, Brooke, over STARS Summer.

00:41:54.440 --> 00:41:56.310
You can actually see that,

00:41:56.310 --> 00:42:00.900
even by graduating pretty
recently, we were...

00:42:00.900 --> 00:42:04.540
This camera quality was
actually the best technology

00:42:04.540 --> 00:42:06.040
that was available.

00:42:06.040 --> 00:42:08.640
It was a few years ago, but
it's kind of incredible.

00:42:09.500 --> 00:42:12.390
You know, science really
does progress very quickly

00:42:12.390 --> 00:42:13.720
and starts (indistinct).

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:14.997
So thank you.

00:42:16.080 --> 00:42:18.530
We were on the bus to the beach.

00:42:18.530 --> 00:42:21.550
Here you can see that we are on a boat.

00:42:21.550 --> 00:42:22.840
You can tell by our posturing

00:42:22.840 --> 00:42:24.840
that we were extremely popular,

00:42:24.840 --> 00:42:26.980
even though we're standing by ourselves,

00:42:26.980 --> 00:42:28.057
and you may be thinking,

00:42:28.057 --> 00:42:31.310
"Man, how did this guy
come to sit before you

00:42:31.310 --> 00:42:33.170
and he's presenting in this panel?"

00:42:33.170 --> 00:42:35.690
Well, a few years later,

00:42:35.690 --> 00:42:39.770
you can have a glow up just
like this boom, we made a baby.

00:42:39.770 --> 00:42:43.720
And so Brooke and I got
married a few months ago,

00:42:43.720 --> 00:42:45.690
August 2020.

00:42:45.690 --> 00:42:48.180
I dunno, we were the, as Mary put it,

00:42:48.180 --> 00:42:51.560
the first STARS crossed
lovers to get married.

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:53.200
Hopefully we're not the last,

00:42:53.200 --> 00:42:55.010
but you know, have fun with it.

00:42:55.010 --> 00:42:55.843
It's good time.

00:42:55.843 --> 00:42:58.750
STARS will, you know, in many ways

00:42:58.750 --> 00:43:03.750
help set you up on the path and
a very fruitful (indistinct)

00:43:03.950 --> 00:43:05.250
Thanks guys for listening.

00:43:06.210 --> 00:43:07.160
- Thank you so much Ben.

00:43:07.160 --> 00:43:07.993
That was terrific.

00:43:07.993 --> 00:43:10.720
Our next speaker is Clarence Agbi,

00:43:10.720 --> 00:43:12.950
a Senior Data Scientist at Brex.

00:43:12.950 --> 00:43:14.300
Clarence.

00:43:14.300 --> 00:43:16.650
- Hello, thanks for the introduction.

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:18.980
My name is Clarence Agbi, class of 2009,

00:43:20.270 --> 00:43:22.510
which is a while ago at this point.

00:43:22.510 --> 00:43:24.250
Majored in electrical engineering.

00:43:24.250 --> 00:43:27.460
I was part of Calhoun
now Grace Hopper College.

00:43:27.460 --> 00:43:30.820
And if you all remember
nothing from this conversation

00:43:30.820 --> 00:43:31.773
or this talk,

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:35.730
I absolutely want you to
remember this one thing

00:43:35.730 --> 00:43:39.340
that, you know, basically
Carissa stole it, already,

00:43:39.340 --> 00:43:41.723
is make your own lane, make your own path,

00:43:43.030 --> 00:43:45.430
and don't be afraid to pivot, right?

00:43:45.430 --> 00:43:47.670
And so one of the things
that I'll, you know,

00:43:47.670 --> 00:43:50.040
kind of talk about is my own path,

00:43:50.040 --> 00:43:52.070
my own journey through STEM

00:43:52.070 --> 00:43:54.550
and how STARS played a role in that.

00:43:54.550 --> 00:43:57.120
For me I'm originally from New York,

00:43:57.120 --> 00:43:59.070
so shout out to any New
Yorkers on the call,

00:43:59.070 --> 00:44:00.360
son of immigrants,

00:44:00.360 --> 00:44:02.983
you know, shout out to all of you as well.

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:05.273
Came to Yale

00:44:05.273 --> 00:44:07.650
and I wasn't really sure
what my path was gonna be.

00:44:07.650 --> 00:44:11.770
I mean, I was into like AP
math, AP science and all of that

00:44:11.770 --> 00:44:13.990
but, you know, I came to Yale

00:44:13.990 --> 00:44:15.717
just to give myself the option of like

00:44:15.717 --> 00:44:17.620
"What if I wanted to be an English major

00:44:17.620 --> 00:44:20.470
or an econ or a Spanish major,"

00:44:20.470 --> 00:44:22.470
'cause these were all
things I was interested in.

00:44:22.470 --> 00:44:25.100
And I would say definitely econ 115,

00:44:25.100 --> 00:44:26.360
is that the introductory course?

00:44:26.360 --> 00:44:29.050
It definitely crushed
all of my hopes of that.

00:44:29.050 --> 00:44:30.793
I think I got a C in that class.

00:44:31.850 --> 00:44:34.110
Quickly learned English was not for me,

00:44:34.110 --> 00:44:37.720
Spanish also not great for me,

00:44:37.720 --> 00:44:40.610
even though that summer
after freshman year

00:44:40.610 --> 00:44:44.250
I worked at a temp agency as
part of Bulldogs in the Rockies

00:44:45.290 --> 00:44:49.180
translating basically
from English to Spanish

00:44:50.940 --> 00:44:52.520
job requirements and things like that.

00:44:52.520 --> 00:44:54.920
So, you must be wondering like, well,

00:44:54.920 --> 00:44:57.833
what does this have to
do with engineering?

00:44:58.740 --> 00:45:01.080
All of this is kind of like
on the journey of things

00:45:01.080 --> 00:45:02.940
that failed for me, right?

00:45:02.940 --> 00:45:07.320
And ultimately I figured out
sometime around sophomore year

00:45:07.320 --> 00:45:09.790
that I was really really into engineering.

00:45:09.790 --> 00:45:13.010
And I think freshman year I took physics

00:45:13.010 --> 00:45:14.150
but it wasn't until I took

00:45:14.150 --> 00:45:17.580
some of the hardcore engineering
classes that I was like

00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:19.510
oh yeah, this is really clicking for me.

00:45:19.510 --> 00:45:21.123
This is really making sense.

00:45:22.570 --> 00:45:25.363
And you know, in that time,

00:45:26.250 --> 00:45:27.250
during sophomore year,

00:45:27.250 --> 00:45:28.837
I think one class that
really stood out for me

00:45:28.837 --> 00:45:32.040
was Professor Cruz's class EE 350.

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:33.920
I don't even know if they
still have that class

00:45:33.920 --> 00:45:37.300
but professor Cruz
essentially ran the digital,

00:45:37.300 --> 00:45:40.340
I think it's the acoustics lab.

00:45:40.340 --> 00:45:42.210
He was really into like bots and acoustics

00:45:42.210 --> 00:45:45.160
but essentially it was all about robotics,

00:45:45.160 --> 00:45:50.160
and how do you use systems to
do very intelligent things.

00:45:51.950 --> 00:45:53.320
And in that class,

00:45:53.320 --> 00:45:56.093
he had us build like a
differential drive robot,

00:45:57.070 --> 00:45:58.860
which I had never done before,

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:00.790
I thought, wow, this is really cool.

00:46:00.790 --> 00:46:02.670
And that really picked my interest

00:46:02.670 --> 00:46:05.410
and got me thinking about grad school.

00:46:05.410 --> 00:46:06.840
The summer after sophomore year,

00:46:06.840 --> 00:46:10.640
I ended up going to a
program similar to STARS,

00:46:10.640 --> 00:46:13.298
it was NSF funded, but
it was at Georgia Tech,

00:46:13.298 --> 00:46:18.147
with predominantly minority
black and brown people, women,

00:46:20.330 --> 00:46:25.290
and it really made room
for me to one, do research

00:46:25.290 --> 00:46:26.753
at an institution and two,

00:46:29.470 --> 00:46:31.700
just figure out how to
get into grad school

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:34.090
'cause it was such an ambiguous process,

00:46:34.090 --> 00:46:38.431
and it just felt like a
big barrier and a big hump.

00:46:38.431 --> 00:46:42.630
So did that, and I would say
that program specifically,

00:46:42.630 --> 00:46:45.780
much like STARS I at Yale,

00:46:45.780 --> 00:46:48.620
really made me wanna be a PhD student.

00:46:48.620 --> 00:46:50.640
I mean, I came back from
my summer in Atlanta

00:46:50.640 --> 00:46:54.230
and I was like research,
where can I do more of it?

00:46:54.230 --> 00:46:55.930
And that's where at that point,

00:46:55.930 --> 00:46:57.697
Dean George kind of
intersect and was like,

00:46:57.697 --> 00:46:59.147
"Have you heard of STARS II?"

00:47:00.530 --> 00:47:02.900
And by the way, I should say
a lot of the STARS I folks

00:47:02.900 --> 00:47:03.820
are very tight knit.

00:47:03.820 --> 00:47:07.110
I kind of have a little bit of FOMO

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:08.540
about not doing that program,

00:47:08.540 --> 00:47:11.270
but you know, I didn't
wanna be in New Haven

00:47:11.270 --> 00:47:13.150
not one moment longer during the summer.

00:47:13.150 --> 00:47:14.750
So (laughs),

00:47:14.750 --> 00:47:15.720
so I left.

00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:17.650
But when I came back,

00:47:17.650 --> 00:47:19.920
like I said, junior
year I put my head down

00:47:19.920 --> 00:47:21.440
and I talked to Professor Coots

00:47:21.440 --> 00:47:23.830
and I was like let's do
some research together.

00:47:23.830 --> 00:47:26.200
And so I applied to STARS
II, was a STARS II student

00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:28.910
and did research for
about a year and a half,

00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:31.473
which eventually turned
into my senior project.

00:47:33.230 --> 00:47:34.240
In that year and a half,

00:47:34.240 --> 00:47:36.130
I learned a lot about ambiguity.

00:47:36.130 --> 00:47:39.550
I learned a lot about scoping projects.

00:47:39.550 --> 00:47:40.920
I trekked.

00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:44.950
I lived at Becton lab and I
trekked often to the FM house

00:47:44.950 --> 00:47:48.440
where I did, you know, I
was normal person, right?

00:47:48.440 --> 00:47:50.500
So I didn't just do research 24/7.

00:47:50.500 --> 00:47:52.890
I was part of like,
you know, gospel choir,

00:47:52.890 --> 00:47:54.710
I was part of stepping out, you know,

00:47:54.710 --> 00:47:56.480
I saw people,

00:47:56.480 --> 00:47:59.660
I had to maintain a social
life and be a real person.

00:47:59.660 --> 00:48:00.883
But on top of that,

00:48:02.380 --> 00:48:04.640
I was also really motivated to do research

00:48:04.640 --> 00:48:05.765
and to get into grad school

00:48:05.765 --> 00:48:08.180
and do all of these wonderful things.

00:48:08.180 --> 00:48:11.640
And I was really gun hold
about sticking on this path

00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:14.180
of research, right?

00:48:14.180 --> 00:48:17.710
So, you know, eventually
things worked out.

00:48:17.710 --> 00:48:18.543
I ended up,

00:48:19.400 --> 00:48:21.400
like I said, it became my senior project.

00:48:21.400 --> 00:48:22.690
You can actually Google it.

00:48:22.690 --> 00:48:24.100
I think the YouTube link is out there,

00:48:24.100 --> 00:48:25.600
you can ask me about it later.

00:48:26.883 --> 00:48:30.180
And I got into, you know,

00:48:30.180 --> 00:48:32.780
something that people won't
often tell you is that

00:48:32.780 --> 00:48:34.890
some of these programs are a
little bit of a crap shoot.

00:48:34.890 --> 00:48:37.830
So I got into two of the
eight graduate programs

00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:39.160
that I applied to,

00:48:39.160 --> 00:48:42.250
and I was lucky enough to
get into Carnegie Mellon

00:48:42.250 --> 00:48:43.930
for my MS/PhD program.

00:48:43.930 --> 00:48:46.180
So, you know, don't feel bad

00:48:46.180 --> 00:48:49.549
if you don't get into all of
the things that you wanna do.

00:48:49.549 --> 00:48:53.090
My motto is to kind of fail
forward and fail often, right?

00:48:53.090 --> 00:48:56.060
And like I said early, you
know, don't be afraid to pivot.

00:48:56.060 --> 00:49:01.060
So I went to CMU, did
hardcore engineering there,

00:49:02.700 --> 00:49:07.020
actually did research in smart
buildings as part of like,

00:49:07.020 --> 00:49:10.420
I was also an NSF
graduate research fellow.

00:49:10.420 --> 00:49:13.990
So I was doing research and how
do we make buildings smarter

00:49:13.990 --> 00:49:18.360
and build really fancy
control systems to do that,

00:49:18.360 --> 00:49:20.680
I was writing proofs for days,

00:49:20.680 --> 00:49:23.460
and ultimately after
five years of doing that,

00:49:23.460 --> 00:49:25.450
you know, I was freaked out.

00:49:25.450 --> 00:49:27.110
I couldn't go any further.

00:49:27.110 --> 00:49:29.860
So, you know, hats off
to you, Dr. Williams,

00:49:29.860 --> 00:49:32.410
and to all the other
doctors that stayed on,

00:49:32.410 --> 00:49:34.980
you know, and did research
and became professors.

00:49:34.980 --> 00:49:37.160
I took a completely different route

00:49:37.160 --> 00:49:40.063
and was like I'm going into industry.

00:49:41.110 --> 00:49:44.320
I became a software engineer
at a defense company

00:49:44.320 --> 00:49:47.590
and then eventually pivoted
into this very brand new thing

00:49:47.590 --> 00:49:48.980
called data science.

00:49:48.980 --> 00:49:50.480
And at the time it was brand new,

00:49:50.480 --> 00:49:51.757
now everybody is kind of like,

00:49:51.757 --> 00:49:54.690
"Oh yeah! Data science, that's the thing."

00:49:54.690 --> 00:49:56.820
But I say all of this to say that,

00:49:56.820 --> 00:50:01.460
in, you know, the process of
me getting to where I am now,

00:50:01.460 --> 00:50:03.820
where I feel like I'm in a job

00:50:03.820 --> 00:50:08.270
that completely suits all of my abilities,

00:50:08.270 --> 00:50:11.070
some of these things
didn't exist at that time.

00:50:11.070 --> 00:50:15.810
And I think it's okay
that it doesn't exist now,

00:50:15.810 --> 00:50:18.320
you know, you might be
built to do something

00:50:18.320 --> 00:50:20.410
that is completely brand new.

00:50:20.410 --> 00:50:24.720
You might not be built
for the traditional path

00:50:24.720 --> 00:50:25.790
or you might not be built

00:50:25.790 --> 00:50:27.220
for the non-traditional path either.

00:50:27.220 --> 00:50:30.140
There's a lot of ambiguity and
stress and imposter syndrome

00:50:30.140 --> 00:50:31.310
and all of that there as well.

00:50:31.310 --> 00:50:34.850
So, you know, definitely feel free to go

00:50:34.850 --> 00:50:36.190
to all the breakout rooms,

00:50:36.190 --> 00:50:38.030
but definitely come to mind,

00:50:38.030 --> 00:50:39.290
if you wanna talk a little bit more,

00:50:39.290 --> 00:50:42.820
I can speak to just about
anything in that experience

00:50:42.820 --> 00:50:46.240
right from, you know, Yale to grad school,

00:50:46.240 --> 00:50:49.233
to what it's like now working
in the engineering world,

00:50:51.050 --> 00:50:53.730
in tech and where to go from there.

00:50:53.730 --> 00:50:56.860
Certainly my career is still taking pivots

00:50:56.860 --> 00:50:58.830
and I'm still making a path for myself,

00:50:58.830 --> 00:51:00.864
so I don't claim to
have it all figured out

00:51:00.864 --> 00:51:02.870
and I don't think any
of the other speakers do

00:51:02.870 --> 00:51:06.360
but certainly we can help
you where you are now

00:51:06.360 --> 00:51:09.650
and you know, work
together on the journey.

00:51:09.650 --> 00:51:11.910
So, thanks for that.

00:51:13.298 --> 00:51:15.610
- Thank you very much Clarence.

00:51:15.610 --> 00:51:18.240
Our next speaker is Raul Navarro,

00:51:18.240 --> 00:51:21.470
Assistant Professor of
Chemistry at Occidental College.

00:51:21.470 --> 00:51:22.303
Raul.

00:51:23.590 --> 00:51:24.720
- Hey everybody.

00:51:24.720 --> 00:51:26.150
My name is Raul Navarro.

00:51:26.150 --> 00:51:29.470
I am currently an assistant professor

00:51:29.470 --> 00:51:31.720
at Occidental College
in Southern California.

00:51:32.820 --> 00:51:34.170
First off, I just wanted to say

00:51:34.170 --> 00:51:37.673
I'm really excited to see
so many familiar faces.

00:51:38.690 --> 00:51:41.273
Clarence, Tina, some of my classmates,

00:51:42.540 --> 00:51:44.840
Dean Chun, who I knew as Master Chun

00:51:44.840 --> 00:51:46.950
of Berkeley College back then,

00:51:46.950 --> 00:51:48.950
Professor Zilm who I
didn't have the pleasure

00:51:48.950 --> 00:51:49.847
of taking a class with

00:51:49.847 --> 00:51:51.320
but I remember your face very well

00:51:51.320 --> 00:51:53.643
spending a lot of time in Sterling.

00:51:55.262 --> 00:51:57.530
So, let's see,

00:51:57.530 --> 00:52:00.060
I guess I'll tell you
a little bit about how

00:52:00.060 --> 00:52:01.460
I got involved with STARS

00:52:01.460 --> 00:52:03.958
and how it led to where I am today.

00:52:03.958 --> 00:52:06.310
You know, it's sort of it is that

00:52:06.310 --> 00:52:08.003
it essentially molded my,

00:52:09.040 --> 00:52:11.570
the person I am today in a lot of ways.

00:52:11.570 --> 00:52:15.110
I started Yale coming from,

00:52:15.110 --> 00:52:16.700
I was born and raised in LA,

00:52:16.700 --> 00:52:19.130
you know, son of immigrant parents

00:52:19.130 --> 00:52:22.220
who had just come from Mexico, right?

00:52:22.220 --> 00:52:25.010
And, you know, they kind
of just came over here

00:52:25.010 --> 00:52:27.120
to give us all these opportunities.

00:52:27.120 --> 00:52:29.560
And so I took that very seriously as a kid

00:52:29.560 --> 00:52:32.770
and I was lucky enough to get into Yale

00:52:32.770 --> 00:52:34.680
thinking I was going to do pre-med.

00:52:34.680 --> 00:52:36.340
Everyone's kind of touched on this idea

00:52:36.340 --> 00:52:38.240
of these meandering paths, right?

00:52:38.240 --> 00:52:40.020
And so that was mine.

00:52:40.020 --> 00:52:42.020
I was definitely pre-med
when I first started.

00:52:42.020 --> 00:52:45.550
I had a general vague sense of, you know,

00:52:45.550 --> 00:52:46.910
I wanted to help people,

00:52:46.910 --> 00:52:50.393
but I don't think I really
understood how then at the time.

00:52:51.620 --> 00:52:53.482
And then I tried the STARS program.

00:52:53.482 --> 00:52:56.182
I remember the end of my
freshman year there was this,

00:52:57.560 --> 00:52:59.400
you know, I saw a flyer for it.

00:52:59.400 --> 00:53:02.650
I was like, well, med schools
look for research experience,

00:53:02.650 --> 00:53:04.250
let's give it a shot.

00:53:04.250 --> 00:53:05.980
And honestly the rest is history.

00:53:05.980 --> 00:53:07.920
I had my first summer research experience.

00:53:07.920 --> 00:53:12.920
Dr. Black was the person who
really helped me out there.

00:53:13.660 --> 00:53:16.110
You know, I'm really embedded to her

00:53:16.110 --> 00:53:17.973
because I still remember the fact

00:53:17.973 --> 00:53:21.370
that it was like a few weeks
before the summer started

00:53:21.370 --> 00:53:23.550
and I still hadn't picked out a lab

00:53:23.550 --> 00:53:25.450
which apparently I was supposed to do.

00:53:26.750 --> 00:53:30.260
And so Dr. Black of course
comes in is, you know,

00:53:30.260 --> 00:53:33.460
essentially hold my hand
through the entire process,

00:53:33.460 --> 00:53:36.000
which I really needed at the time

00:53:36.000 --> 00:53:37.003
'cause I had no clue what I was doing,

00:53:37.003 --> 00:53:39.780
and helped me figure out
a lab, a chemistry lab.

00:53:39.780 --> 00:53:42.670
I still remember the
first day of my time there

00:53:42.670 --> 00:53:44.850
because it was such a
crazy experience for me.

00:53:44.850 --> 00:53:46.620
I'd never seen anything like it.

00:53:46.620 --> 00:53:48.993
Grad school to me was
such a foreign thing.

00:53:50.070 --> 00:53:52.890
And I was there in that
first day thinking like wow,

00:53:52.890 --> 00:53:55.330
I was in a lab and just an amazement

00:53:55.330 --> 00:53:57.570
that I could do something
like that and get paid for it.

00:53:57.570 --> 00:54:00.303
That to me was just such a crazy concept.

00:54:01.360 --> 00:54:02.635
And so, you know, the rest is history.

00:54:02.635 --> 00:54:04.590
I enjoyed my time there.

00:54:04.590 --> 00:54:05.490
It was tough.

00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:08.830
You know, learning a lot of the
stuff you do as a researcher

00:54:08.830 --> 00:54:11.483
when you had just taken general chemistry.

00:54:14.070 --> 00:54:16.720
But I got a lot of great
training in that first summer.

00:54:16.720 --> 00:54:19.900
I then took organic chemistry,
my second year of class

00:54:19.900 --> 00:54:21.970
that I think a lot of people dread taking.

00:54:21.970 --> 00:54:24.650
And I certainly wasn't a
big fan my first semester

00:54:24.650 --> 00:54:28.500
but once I think I devoted more time to it

00:54:28.500 --> 00:54:31.410
and went to office hours
and got a lot of help for it

00:54:31.410 --> 00:54:33.800
I became really good at it

00:54:33.800 --> 00:54:36.380
and really wanted to do research in it.

00:54:36.380 --> 00:54:39.770
And so then I approached
my professor at the time,

00:54:39.770 --> 00:54:41.700
let me do research,
he's no longer at Yale,

00:54:41.700 --> 00:54:45.610
but he graciously let me be in his lab.

00:54:45.610 --> 00:54:46.820
And that's when I really

00:54:46.820 --> 00:54:49.410
I think found that the
great intersection of,

00:54:49.410 --> 00:54:52.180
for me, the kind of
research that I wanted to do

00:54:52.180 --> 00:54:54.210
where it kind of overlapped
with a lot of my interests.

00:54:54.210 --> 00:54:56.950
So for me, it was kind of
about building molecules

00:54:56.950 --> 00:54:58.730
and that I thought was really cool.

00:54:58.730 --> 00:55:02.030
So I did that for the
rest of my time at Yale.

00:55:02.030 --> 00:55:03.610
It was more or less two years

00:55:03.610 --> 00:55:06.573
that I was in Professor
Glenn Micalizio's lab,

00:55:08.334 --> 00:55:11.700
and the decision to apply
to grad school for me

00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:13.328
wasn't necessarily a conscious one.

00:55:13.328 --> 00:55:16.650
I reached a point in my junior year

00:55:16.650 --> 00:55:18.420
where I was thinking about grad schools

00:55:18.420 --> 00:55:20.300
without having sort of
thought about the fact

00:55:20.300 --> 00:55:23.160
that I was no longer
like a pre-med student

00:55:23.160 --> 00:55:25.470
and was actively thinking about like,

00:55:25.470 --> 00:55:26.840
I'm gonna be in graduate school next.

00:55:26.840 --> 00:55:28.570
And so for me it was kind of a decision

00:55:28.570 --> 00:55:31.513
that was kind of made for me in that way.

00:55:33.880 --> 00:55:36.570
And so, let's see, I...

00:55:37.440 --> 00:55:39.183
So after Yale, you know, I
also applied to grad school.

00:55:39.183 --> 00:55:41.340
Clarence mentioned, you know,

00:55:41.340 --> 00:55:43.170
really grad school really
is kind of a crapshoot.

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:45.520
You'll get into places and
you're gonna think like,

00:55:45.520 --> 00:55:47.340
I got in here but I didn't get it here.

00:55:47.340 --> 00:55:49.600
It's like it doesn't make any sense.

00:55:49.600 --> 00:55:50.890
But that's just how it goes, right?

00:55:50.890 --> 00:55:55.600
So I was excited to get into Caltech.

00:55:55.600 --> 00:55:57.410
And so I went back to...

00:55:57.410 --> 00:56:00.700
And I will also kind of
missing, being in California,

00:56:00.700 --> 00:56:01.533
having spent...

00:56:01.533 --> 00:56:04.100
I love Yale but I didn't love the weather.

00:56:04.100 --> 00:56:06.350
This is the summary of
my four years at Yale.

00:56:07.480 --> 00:56:11.510
So I came back to do my PhD at
Caltech where I did chemistry

00:56:13.480 --> 00:56:15.050
and you know, my experience there,

00:56:15.050 --> 00:56:17.780
I'm happy to talk about it
in the breakout sessions,

00:56:17.780 --> 00:56:18.970
I had a great time there.

00:56:18.970 --> 00:56:21.310
I, you know, I got a chance to really dive

00:56:21.310 --> 00:56:23.150
into more organic chemistry research

00:56:23.150 --> 00:56:26.550
thinking about making these,
you know, what we refer to as

00:56:26.550 --> 00:56:28.400
these, you know, natural products,

00:56:28.400 --> 00:56:30.190
molecules you can isolate from nature

00:56:30.190 --> 00:56:32.210
that have some interesting
biological properties,

00:56:32.210 --> 00:56:34.283
potentially treat some human disease.

00:56:36.120 --> 00:56:37.890
Where I really got a
chance to explore that

00:56:37.890 --> 00:56:40.870
and also really develop
my senses of identity

00:56:40.870 --> 00:56:43.320
that has accumulated all
up at Yale, to be honest.

00:56:43.320 --> 00:56:48.300
And so, you know, I think Aida mentioned,

00:56:48.300 --> 00:56:50.662
you know, starting to do
things outside of the research

00:56:50.662 --> 00:56:54.040
(indistinct) and I think for
me that was also equally true.

00:56:54.040 --> 00:56:59.040
I got a chance to really
start to develop a sense of,

00:56:59.540 --> 00:57:04.120
I guess, balance between
research and celebrating who I am

00:57:04.120 --> 00:57:05.490
and pursuing those interests.

00:57:05.490 --> 00:57:09.590
And so I became really
involved with reviving

00:57:09.590 --> 00:57:14.590
and really establishing the
LGBT group on campus at Caltech,

00:57:15.060 --> 00:57:17.060
which was really a rewarding experience.

00:57:19.203 --> 00:57:21.414
And so, you know, coming
out of grad school,

00:57:21.414 --> 00:57:23.198
I'm running out of the time here,

00:57:23.198 --> 00:57:25.083
I did a post-doc.

00:57:26.029 --> 00:57:28.770
And so I went up to Stanford University

00:57:28.770 --> 00:57:30.860
to do some research in chemical biology,

00:57:30.860 --> 00:57:33.670
trying something a little bit different,

00:57:33.670 --> 00:57:35.780
applying some of the
compounds that I, you know,

00:57:35.780 --> 00:57:37.260
developed or made in grad school

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:39.713
and thinking more broadly about how to,

00:57:40.749 --> 00:57:43.070
how they can be used to study

00:57:43.070 --> 00:57:44.620
some sort of biological function.

00:57:44.620 --> 00:57:48.060
So I went up there and
spent three or four years,

00:57:48.060 --> 00:57:50.233
almost four years as a post-doc.

00:57:52.490 --> 00:57:55.064
Also happy to unpack that experience

00:57:55.064 --> 00:57:56.690
Each of these transitions,

00:57:56.690 --> 00:57:59.240
I think we all, you know,
you get thrown for a loop

00:57:59.240 --> 00:58:00.210
in what you're doing

00:58:00.210 --> 00:58:03.860
and in each one of these I
think was super challenging

00:58:03.860 --> 00:58:05.400
for me in a lot of ways.

00:58:05.400 --> 00:58:08.050
And really what it came down to for me

00:58:08.050 --> 00:58:10.610
was just starting to develop
a sense of community, right?

00:58:10.610 --> 00:58:12.840
I think Aida mentioned, I
forget who else mentioned,

00:58:12.840 --> 00:58:17.080
as you go up, as you
climb beyond undergrad

00:58:17.080 --> 00:58:21.300
your community starts to
get noticeably smaller,

00:58:21.300 --> 00:58:24.550
almost exponentially smaller
as you climb each rank.

00:58:24.550 --> 00:58:26.720
And so it becomes a lot more important

00:58:26.720 --> 00:58:28.510
to make sure you establish
those connections

00:58:28.510 --> 00:58:32.540
to make sure you recognize that
you're not doing this alone,

00:58:32.540 --> 00:58:35.040
that you're not kind of swimming
in your head by yourself

00:58:35.040 --> 00:58:37.520
which I often find myself doing.

00:58:37.520 --> 00:58:39.060
Navigating imposter syndrome,

00:58:39.060 --> 00:58:42.630
is one of those things
that it's very challenging

00:58:42.630 --> 00:58:44.220
and it never really leaves you.

00:58:44.220 --> 00:58:46.370
And so it's good to
develop good techniques

00:58:47.460 --> 00:58:49.070
including reaching out to people.

00:58:49.070 --> 00:58:52.261
And so now I get a chance to,
do a little bit of research

00:58:52.261 --> 00:58:54.580
at a liberal arts institution.

00:58:54.580 --> 00:58:57.530
And so we're really focused on teaching.

00:58:57.530 --> 00:58:58.727
I get to do a lot of teaching

00:58:58.727 --> 00:59:00.780
and really think about
really creative ways

00:59:00.780 --> 00:59:03.770
to deliver classes like organic chemistry.

00:59:03.770 --> 00:59:07.000
I have a group of
undergraduate researchers.

00:59:07.000 --> 00:59:08.600
And so that's been really rewarding.

00:59:08.600 --> 00:59:12.520
Something I got to do at
Yale was STARS I, STARS II.

00:59:12.520 --> 00:59:14.310
I did all of the STARS things basically

00:59:14.310 --> 00:59:16.150
since the end of my freshman year,

00:59:16.150 --> 00:59:18.860
I got a chance to be a
facilitator and tutor people

00:59:18.860 --> 00:59:21.150
and that's something that
I think really inspired me

00:59:21.150 --> 00:59:24.650
to keep doing that sort
of kind of work today.

00:59:24.650 --> 00:59:27.940
And then I guess one last
thing in terms of advice

00:59:27.940 --> 00:59:30.480
I think to give to the younger students is

00:59:32.590 --> 00:59:33.660
I would say to make sure

00:59:33.660 --> 00:59:36.000
you let yourself take opportunities.

00:59:36.000 --> 00:59:38.320
So it's one thing to take an opportunity

00:59:38.320 --> 00:59:39.780
and people tell you that,

00:59:39.780 --> 00:59:43.357
but it's a whole nother to be
in a mindset where you can say

00:59:43.357 --> 00:59:45.490
"I can do that."

00:59:45.490 --> 00:59:48.910
And I think I struggled a lot
with that as an undergrad,

00:59:48.910 --> 00:59:50.800
always kind of second guessing myself

00:59:50.800 --> 00:59:54.260
and not being sure
that's not really for me.

00:59:54.260 --> 00:59:56.810
Kind of shutting it down
before I even consider it.

00:59:58.160 --> 01:00:00.800
And so that's sort of one
I think mental barrier

01:00:00.800 --> 01:00:03.090
that I took a lot of time to overcome.

01:00:03.090 --> 01:00:04.817
And so this, I think,

01:00:04.817 --> 01:00:07.700
you have a lot of opportunities
while you're at Yale,

01:00:07.700 --> 01:00:10.090
and I think it's just
important to make sure

01:00:10.090 --> 01:00:13.950
that you put yourself in a
position where you can say yes,

01:00:13.950 --> 01:00:14.783
I can try that,

01:00:14.783 --> 01:00:17.070
and yes, I can do it as well.

01:00:17.070 --> 01:00:17.903
I'm happy to...

01:00:17.903 --> 01:00:19.207
I'm looking forward to
chatting with all of you

01:00:19.207 --> 01:00:21.310
in the breakout sessions.

01:00:21.310 --> 01:00:22.640
Thank you.

01:00:22.640 --> 01:00:24.570
- Thank you very much Raul.

01:00:24.570 --> 01:00:27.323
Our next speaker is Akpanoluo Etteh,

01:00:28.330 --> 01:00:30.273
a Data Scientist and CEO.

01:00:32.640 --> 01:00:35.560
- Excellent. Thank you so much Sandy.

01:00:35.560 --> 01:00:38.880
By the way, I've been
messaging Dean George

01:00:38.880 --> 01:00:41.370
and she says to say hi.

01:00:41.370 --> 01:00:44.448
She also says to give a hug to Clarence.

01:00:44.448 --> 01:00:46.890
(laughs)

01:00:46.890 --> 01:00:49.607
Yes, once again my name is Akpanoluo.

01:00:50.472 --> 01:00:55.472
I am a data scientist who
works as analytics lead

01:00:57.040 --> 01:01:00.620
at Music Tech Startup band camp,

01:01:00.620 --> 01:01:04.237
which helps support artists, musicians,

01:01:04.237 --> 01:01:07.790
in actually making money
for their music online

01:01:07.790 --> 01:01:09.780
in comparison to Spotify,

01:01:09.780 --> 01:01:11.900
but we'll not get into that.

01:01:11.900 --> 01:01:16.900
And I'm also the CEO and Chief
Curator of a music community

01:01:16.990 --> 01:01:19.950
that I founded four years
ago called Sound Shop,

01:01:19.950 --> 01:01:24.950
which is effectively a
ted talk meets a concert.

01:01:25.730 --> 01:01:28.500
Musicians talk about their music

01:01:28.500 --> 01:01:30.060
and they perform their music

01:01:30.060 --> 01:01:32.910
and then have an intellectual conversation

01:01:32.910 --> 01:01:35.086
with the audience.

01:01:35.086 --> 01:01:39.573
So in terms of what I want
to talk to you guys about,

01:01:40.810 --> 01:01:42.760
I would say that the important thing

01:01:42.760 --> 01:01:45.597
that I want to impart on
you is two things actually.

01:01:45.597 --> 01:01:46.630
The first is

01:01:46.630 --> 01:01:49.640
that you should pursue
your intellectual curiosity

01:01:49.640 --> 01:01:51.330
wherever that leads,

01:01:51.330 --> 01:01:54.840
even if it leads you away
from what you perceived

01:01:54.840 --> 01:01:56.520
to be your path.

01:01:56.520 --> 01:01:58.970
And the other thing would be that,

01:01:58.970 --> 01:02:01.640
even if you do go far a field

01:02:01.640 --> 01:02:03.650
from what you expected your path to be,

01:02:03.650 --> 01:02:06.330
that the time that you spent prior

01:02:06.330 --> 01:02:08.560
is not wasted and that actually, you know,

01:02:08.560 --> 01:02:09.840
you bring all of it with you

01:02:09.840 --> 01:02:14.320
and you're actually able
to re reorient those skills

01:02:14.320 --> 01:02:17.060
in powerful ways in new environments.

01:02:17.060 --> 01:02:21.240
So I just want to kind of
kick this off metaphorically

01:02:21.240 --> 01:02:23.353
with this painting that I painted.

01:02:23.353 --> 01:02:28.353
I painted this in high school,

01:02:28.980 --> 01:02:31.330
my senior year of high school.

01:02:31.330 --> 01:02:33.030
So my mom's a painter,

01:02:33.030 --> 01:02:38.030
and she's also an art
professor and art historian.

01:02:39.640 --> 01:02:44.640
And she also encouraged
me to go into the sciences

01:02:44.680 --> 01:02:46.780
like at the exclusion of the arts.

01:02:46.780 --> 01:02:50.030
So there's this kind of like
constant dichotomy in me

01:02:50.030 --> 01:02:52.570
around the arts versus the sciences.

01:02:52.570 --> 01:02:56.990
And in conjunction with that,

01:02:56.990 --> 01:02:58.790
you know, when I started at Yale

01:02:58.790 --> 01:03:00.360
I was deciding between...

01:03:02.760 --> 01:03:04.720
I knew that I wanted to do neuroscience

01:03:04.720 --> 01:03:06.790
but I was trying to decide
between whether I wanted

01:03:06.790 --> 01:03:09.240
to be on the more bio side of things

01:03:09.240 --> 01:03:10.740
or the psych side of things.

01:03:10.740 --> 01:03:14.550
And so being able to,
you know, spend a summer

01:03:14.550 --> 01:03:18.700
in a neurobiology lab up at KBT

01:03:18.700 --> 01:03:22.460
was very influential in sort
of helping me realize that,

01:03:22.460 --> 01:03:24.690
you know, I want to go more
on the psych side of things

01:03:24.690 --> 01:03:28.290
rather than the biology side of things.

01:03:28.290 --> 01:03:30.230
But, you know, as I
continued down that path

01:03:30.230 --> 01:03:31.730
I was thinking of becoming a doctor

01:03:31.730 --> 01:03:33.280
and in the end,

01:03:33.280 --> 01:03:35.810
you know, I decided that
that was not what I wanted.

01:03:35.810 --> 01:03:37.300
And I realized ultimately

01:03:37.300 --> 01:03:39.410
it was what my mom wanted me to do

01:03:39.410 --> 01:03:41.630
and there's really no compromising

01:03:41.630 --> 01:03:43.370
between what you want to do

01:03:43.370 --> 01:03:46.260
and what your parents want you to do.

01:03:46.260 --> 01:03:48.220
And I tried to convince myself otherwise,

01:03:48.220 --> 01:03:50.850
but, you know, right before I graduated,

01:03:50.850 --> 01:03:52.097
I called my mom and was like,

01:03:52.097 --> 01:03:54.650
"Hey, I don't wanna become a doctor."

01:03:54.650 --> 01:03:55.483
She's like, "What are you gonna do?"

01:03:55.483 --> 01:03:58.013
I'm like, "I'm gonna go into advertising."

01:03:58.013 --> 01:03:59.909
And my mom was like, "What?"

01:03:59.909 --> 01:04:02.180
(laughs)

01:04:02.180 --> 01:04:05.020
But, you know, I decided
to go that direction

01:04:05.020 --> 01:04:07.160
because I basically saw that.

01:04:07.160 --> 01:04:10.116
I figured that, you know, my
background in neuroscience

01:04:10.116 --> 01:04:13.450
and psychology would be
useful for advertising.

01:04:13.450 --> 01:04:14.886
To the degree that's true

01:04:14.886 --> 01:04:17.740
in an entry level position in advertising,

01:04:17.740 --> 01:04:21.300
not really necessarily the case,

01:04:21.300 --> 01:04:25.830
but you know, my interest in STEM

01:04:25.830 --> 01:04:28.270
and especially the
technology side of things

01:04:28.270 --> 01:04:31.680
always saw me doing things
that were more technical

01:04:31.680 --> 01:04:33.510
in any role that I was in.

01:04:33.510 --> 01:04:37.840
And also the presentation
skills that I gained

01:04:37.840 --> 01:04:42.840
through STARS and cultivated
through my research background

01:04:42.980 --> 01:04:46.590
also proved hugely
instrumental in my journey.

01:04:46.590 --> 01:04:50.897
And so, you know, as time
moved along, I moved,

01:04:50.897 --> 01:04:53.310
and as I moved up in
the advertising world,

01:04:53.310 --> 01:04:54.560
I became more and more technical.

01:04:54.560 --> 01:04:58.060
So becoming a data analyst,
becoming then a data engineer

01:04:58.060 --> 01:05:00.470
and ultimately a data scientist,

01:05:00.470 --> 01:05:04.730
and all of that, I owe to,
you know, the STEM background

01:05:04.730 --> 01:05:05.880
that I have.

01:05:05.880 --> 01:05:08.140
And then along the way,

01:05:08.140 --> 01:05:11.420
you know, mentorship has
always been important to me,

01:05:11.420 --> 01:05:13.050
both within STEM,

01:05:13.050 --> 01:05:14.610
sorry, both within STARS,

01:05:14.610 --> 01:05:17.610
and then, throughout my
time as an undergrad.

01:05:17.610 --> 01:05:20.490
And so as I sort of moved up in the world,

01:05:20.490 --> 01:05:21.520
I have been,

01:05:21.520 --> 01:05:22.870
you know, I've been mentoring people.

01:05:22.870 --> 01:05:26.470
I've taken people who work
in the advertising business

01:05:26.470 --> 01:05:28.660
and they're more client facing
and they're less technical,

01:05:28.660 --> 01:05:31.230
and I kind of mentor them
into becoming technical.

01:05:31.230 --> 01:05:33.430
So I've been able to have, you know,

01:05:33.430 --> 01:05:35.720
a huge impact on the people around me.

01:05:35.720 --> 01:05:38.720
And so, you know, I kind of
had this narrative in myself

01:05:38.720 --> 01:05:41.170
as I was moving up the ranks that like,

01:05:41.170 --> 01:05:44.550
oh, like all the stuff that I did before

01:05:44.550 --> 01:05:45.530
was not really useful,

01:05:45.530 --> 01:05:48.200
and why did I spend all that
time doing pre-med stuff.

01:05:48.200 --> 01:05:50.730
But I think me being invited back to this,

01:05:50.730 --> 01:05:53.210
has actually made me reflect on how

01:05:53.210 --> 01:05:56.100
it's actually persisted with
me, which is really cool.

01:05:56.100 --> 01:05:59.273
And then, I know I'm over time,

01:05:59.273 --> 01:06:01.730
I actually timed myself
so that I wouldn't,

01:06:01.730 --> 01:06:05.100
but the final thing is, you
know, my music community,

01:06:05.100 --> 01:06:05.980
you know, you think like,

01:06:05.980 --> 01:06:07.840
oh, you're doing this
music curation thing.

01:06:07.840 --> 01:06:11.320
Like how does that relate
to your past background?

01:06:11.320 --> 01:06:14.010
And for the longest time,
it didn't really relate

01:06:14.010 --> 01:06:15.700
or at least I didn't see it as relating,

01:06:15.700 --> 01:06:18.130
but I met this guy named Kyle Mengelkamp

01:06:18.130 --> 01:06:20.410
who is a stroke survivor.

01:06:20.410 --> 01:06:22.770
He had a massive stroke at age 11,

01:06:22.770 --> 01:06:25.070
complete retrograde amnesia,

01:06:25.070 --> 01:06:27.230
had to rehabilitate himself from scratch,

01:06:27.230 --> 01:06:29.539
and now he's 20 years on from that stroke,

01:06:29.539 --> 01:06:32.400
and he runs a brain community

01:06:32.400 --> 01:06:35.170
where he brings together academics

01:06:35.170 --> 01:06:37.930
and survivors of traumatic brain injuries

01:06:37.930 --> 01:06:39.880
and they're all talking about the brain.

01:06:39.880 --> 01:06:42.900
They're all like, you know,
mutually supporting each other.

01:06:42.900 --> 01:06:47.060
And so he and I now do a
lot of collaborative events

01:06:47.060 --> 01:06:48.570
around music in the brain.

01:06:48.570 --> 01:06:50.140
And it's kind of like, you know,

01:06:50.140 --> 01:06:53.910
it's kind of like the book
Musicophilia as like an event.

01:06:53.910 --> 01:06:58.270
So we'll bring together
clinical psychologists

01:06:58.270 --> 01:07:02.192
who run an institution around music

01:07:02.192 --> 01:07:03.760
at Berkeley School of Music,

01:07:03.760 --> 01:07:06.670
or we'll bring in like a psych academic

01:07:06.670 --> 01:07:09.280
who's doing research on music.

01:07:09.280 --> 01:07:12.137
Or, you know, practitioners
at the intersection of music

01:07:12.137 --> 01:07:13.030
and the brain.

01:07:13.030 --> 01:07:14.121
And in one of those events,

01:07:14.121 --> 01:07:17.040
I actually gave a talk,

01:07:17.040 --> 01:07:20.160
where I basically articulated my theory

01:07:20.160 --> 01:07:23.330
that music is the baseline skill

01:07:23.330 --> 01:07:25.100
upon which language is built.

01:07:25.100 --> 01:07:26.660
And I supported that using evidence

01:07:26.660 --> 01:07:30.793
from linguistics neuroscience,

01:07:32.648 --> 01:07:36.680
what was it? Evolutionary
biology, clinical psychology,

01:07:36.680 --> 01:07:40.090
and so I'm now in a
place where I'm like able

01:07:40.090 --> 01:07:44.750
to bring the STEM background
into what once seemed

01:07:44.750 --> 01:07:48.000
like a more sort of like, you know,

01:07:48.000 --> 01:07:51.300
fully arts oriented sort of thing.

01:07:51.300 --> 01:07:54.210
So back to bringing it all full circle,

01:07:54.210 --> 01:07:55.350
you can have it all,

01:07:55.350 --> 01:08:00.110
you can make the arts and
the sciences live together.

01:08:00.110 --> 01:08:04.320
And so that's what I just
wanna convey to everyone.

01:08:04.320 --> 01:08:05.480
Thank you.

01:08:05.480 --> 01:08:08.547
- Thank you very much Akpanoluo.

01:08:08.547 --> 01:08:11.180
Our next speaker is Tina Liu,

01:08:11.180 --> 01:08:13.300
a research specialist at UC Berkeley.

01:08:13.300 --> 01:08:14.133
Tina.

01:08:15.210 --> 01:08:17.860
- All right, so first of
all, thanks for inviting me.

01:08:18.810 --> 01:08:21.130
It's great to see all these alums doing

01:08:21.130 --> 01:08:23.850
all these amazing things at this point

01:08:23.850 --> 01:08:25.900
at all different sort of
stages of their career.

01:08:25.900 --> 01:08:28.060
So thanks a lot for
this opportunity to come

01:08:28.060 --> 01:08:31.060
and join this event today.

01:08:31.060 --> 01:08:32.990
So I'm gonna be talking a little bit

01:08:32.990 --> 01:08:36.260
about kind of my journey at Yale actually,

01:08:36.260 --> 01:08:38.440
and also a bit after,

01:08:38.440 --> 01:08:41.260
mainly focusing on one
particular recent kind of work

01:08:41.260 --> 01:08:43.150
that I'm doing on RNA diagnostics,

01:08:43.150 --> 01:08:44.360
related to COVID,

01:08:44.360 --> 01:08:46.380
but I'll also give sort
of just short vignettes

01:08:46.380 --> 01:08:49.490
of some of the past work
that I've also done before.

01:08:49.490 --> 01:08:51.120
So as Sandy was saying,

01:08:51.120 --> 01:08:52.800
I'm currently a Research
Specialist at Doudna Lab

01:08:52.800 --> 01:08:54.340
where I actually just,

01:08:54.340 --> 01:08:56.930
with a postdoc until very recently.

01:08:56.930 --> 01:09:00.450
I was class of 2008 in
the STARS II program

01:09:00.450 --> 01:09:02.023
in Lynne Regan's lab and MB&amp;B.

01:09:03.363 --> 01:09:05.923
And I think one sort of, you know,

01:09:07.168 --> 01:09:08.350
this event really made me think about

01:09:08.350 --> 01:09:12.160
what sort of is the common theme
that connects my past work.

01:09:12.160 --> 01:09:14.257
'Cause I've worked on a couple,
several different areas,

01:09:14.257 --> 01:09:16.250
and I really think that
one thing that drives

01:09:16.250 --> 01:09:19.340
my sort of interest in research
is really understanding life

01:09:19.340 --> 01:09:20.510
at the molecular level.

01:09:20.510 --> 01:09:24.490
So, you know, obviously a
whole multicellular organism

01:09:24.490 --> 01:09:25.650
is composed of cells,

01:09:25.650 --> 01:09:26.990
and then if you zoom in more

01:09:26.990 --> 01:09:30.500
it has these complex
intracellular structures

01:09:30.500 --> 01:09:33.100
called organelles, and that
when you further break that down

01:09:33.100 --> 01:09:34.350
into macromolecules,

01:09:34.350 --> 01:09:38.930
which include proteins,
sugars, DNA, RNA, lipids,

01:09:38.930 --> 01:09:42.150
that all kind of enable life to exist

01:09:42.150 --> 01:09:45.090
which is just kind of an
amazing thoughts in me.

01:09:45.090 --> 01:09:47.460
And so, I worked in a
couple of different areas.

01:09:47.460 --> 01:09:49.010
One is engineering proteins

01:09:49.010 --> 01:09:51.270
which are one type of macromolecule.

01:09:51.270 --> 01:09:52.860
I'm looking at membrane proteins,

01:09:52.860 --> 01:09:55.090
so the connection between
proteins and lipids.

01:09:55.090 --> 01:09:56.720
So two different types of molecules,

01:09:56.720 --> 01:09:58.440
and then more recently,

01:09:58.440 --> 01:10:01.647
harnessing a type of proteins
called CRISPR-Cas nucleases

01:10:01.647 --> 01:10:02.920
for RNA detection.

01:10:02.920 --> 01:10:05.090
So again, looking at two
different interactions

01:10:05.090 --> 01:10:06.950
between two different
types of macromolecules

01:10:06.950 --> 01:10:08.550
and harnessing them
for different functions

01:10:08.550 --> 01:10:12.070
outside of their endogenous function.

01:10:12.070 --> 01:10:15.510
And so at Yale, what I was
doing at STARS II program

01:10:15.510 --> 01:10:18.960
I was in Lynne Regan's
labs, she's since left Yale,

01:10:18.960 --> 01:10:20.990
she's now at university of Edinburgh,

01:10:20.990 --> 01:10:23.220
but she was a professor
in the MB&amp;B department.

01:10:23.220 --> 01:10:28.220
And she gave me this project
to try to design new proteins

01:10:28.400 --> 01:10:31.810
based on this TPR protein,
which is a natural protein,

01:10:31.810 --> 01:10:33.770
but we wanted to give it new properties

01:10:33.770 --> 01:10:35.420
so that it could bind to a novel target

01:10:35.420 --> 01:10:37.200
or any target that you want.

01:10:37.200 --> 01:10:39.283
And then one of the examples
where the value of doing that

01:10:39.283 --> 01:10:42.280
is that when you use
those design proteins here

01:10:43.260 --> 01:10:45.910
to target any pathway in a cell.

01:10:45.910 --> 01:10:46.810
So in this case,

01:10:46.810 --> 01:10:51.100
the design protein that I was
actually a part of designing

01:10:51.100 --> 01:10:53.390
and engineering, we could
put into yeast cells

01:10:53.390 --> 01:10:56.860
and we could actually
inhibit the specific pathway

01:10:56.860 --> 01:11:00.170
that was the protein bound was a part of.

01:11:00.170 --> 01:11:04.840
And so that actually led
to my first, you know,

01:11:04.840 --> 01:11:07.370
co-first author paper
from the STARS Program

01:11:07.370 --> 01:11:08.930
was the work that I did in Lynne's lab

01:11:08.930 --> 01:11:10.680
which is super exciting as an undergrad.

01:11:10.680 --> 01:11:12.890
It's not something that I
expected that at the time

01:11:12.890 --> 01:11:15.660
but it was amazing to just
have that continuous support

01:11:15.660 --> 01:11:20.660
from STARS to start a research
project in my second semester

01:11:21.410 --> 01:11:22.640
of freshman year and actually get it

01:11:22.640 --> 01:11:24.580
to the point where we
could actually present it

01:11:24.580 --> 01:11:26.350
and share it with the
scientific community.

01:11:26.350 --> 01:11:30.233
So that was really, really
amazing experience starting off.

01:11:31.210 --> 01:11:35.690
Then after Yale, I went to
Harvard for in the BBS program,

01:11:35.690 --> 01:11:37.850
Biological and medical sciences,

01:11:37.850 --> 01:11:39.810
where I really wanted to still work

01:11:39.810 --> 01:11:42.340
on molecular interactions and events

01:11:42.340 --> 01:11:43.700
but understand how they happened

01:11:43.700 --> 01:11:45.970
in the context of cellular pathways.

01:11:45.970 --> 01:11:48.440
And so I joined Tom Rapoport's lab,

01:11:48.440 --> 01:11:51.830
that worked on how organelles
in the cell are shaped

01:11:51.830 --> 01:11:53.240
and how the proteins,

01:11:53.240 --> 01:11:54.477
so this particular family of protein

01:11:54.477 --> 01:11:55.580
called Atlastin proteins,

01:11:55.580 --> 01:11:56.810
how they interact with each other

01:11:56.810 --> 01:12:00.150
and also with the membrane
to fuse membranes together.

01:12:00.150 --> 01:12:02.780
And that it turns out to
be a key sort of event

01:12:02.780 --> 01:12:04.190
in the formation of organelles,

01:12:04.190 --> 01:12:08.400
which as many of you might've already know

01:12:08.400 --> 01:12:12.540
are sort of the internal
compartments of rooms of a cell.

01:12:12.540 --> 01:12:14.290
If you think of a cell as
a building and, you know,

01:12:14.290 --> 01:12:15.720
the kitchen having a specialized function

01:12:15.720 --> 01:12:17.470
and the bedroom, living room and so on.

01:12:17.470 --> 01:12:19.320
So the organelles are kind of see,

01:12:19.320 --> 01:12:21.970
had those compartmentalize
functions inside the cell.

01:12:23.310 --> 01:12:27.410
And then after this, I went
to Jennifer Doudna's lab

01:12:27.410 --> 01:12:28.473
at UC Berkeley,

01:12:30.080 --> 01:12:32.430
and her lab works on CRISPR-Cas systems

01:12:32.430 --> 01:12:35.610
which are actually
bacterial immune systems,

01:12:35.610 --> 01:12:38.906
and these immune systems use
these protein RNA complex

01:12:38.906 --> 01:12:41.120
called RNA-guided
nucleases that protect them

01:12:41.120 --> 01:12:44.180
against invading viruses.

01:12:44.180 --> 01:12:46.410
And one sort of key step, again,

01:12:46.410 --> 01:12:48.350
if we look down to the molecular level

01:12:48.350 --> 01:12:51.660
is that this immune pathway

01:12:51.660 --> 01:12:54.030
requires the use of
these protein RNA factors

01:12:54.030 --> 01:12:58.530
to really enact immunity essentially.

01:12:58.530 --> 01:13:01.630
And so, as some of you
might've heard before us,

01:13:01.630 --> 01:13:03.320
many of these types of nucleases

01:13:03.320 --> 01:13:06.100
can be hardest for DNA
or RNA editing in cells.

01:13:06.100 --> 01:13:08.860
So this is, you know, wide sort of ranging

01:13:08.860 --> 01:13:11.270
of the applications that
are already kind of in use.

01:13:11.270 --> 01:13:12.740
So there's genome editing,

01:13:12.740 --> 01:13:15.310
there's imaging of DNA inside cells,

01:13:15.310 --> 01:13:17.580
activating repressing certain genes

01:13:17.580 --> 01:13:20.530
as well as RNA imaging and
editing and knock down.

01:13:20.530 --> 01:13:22.820
And it's one of the kinds of values,

01:13:22.820 --> 01:13:23.950
value of CRISPR-Cas,

01:13:23.950 --> 01:13:26.050
is that it's not that it is the first time

01:13:26.050 --> 01:13:27.970
that DNA editing has been achieved,

01:13:27.970 --> 01:13:30.440
but it's a tool that's really
easy for people to use.

01:13:30.440 --> 01:13:31.930
So it really democratizes, you know,

01:13:31.930 --> 01:13:33.593
manipulation in biology.

01:13:35.550 --> 01:13:37.346
But the thing that I ended up working on

01:13:37.346 --> 01:13:39.100
actually this past year,

01:13:39.100 --> 01:13:43.010
given that the pandemic
started last spring

01:13:43.010 --> 01:13:44.760
or early last year,

01:13:44.760 --> 01:13:47.320
a lot of labs on campus
really tried to think about

01:13:47.320 --> 01:13:50.020
what we could do to help.

01:13:50.020 --> 01:13:53.980
And one sort of area of
need was, of course testing.

01:13:53.980 --> 01:13:56.590
And so we, a couple of
labs in our building

01:13:56.590 --> 01:13:57.423
actually got together

01:13:57.423 --> 01:13:59.080
and really just brainstorm lots of ideas

01:13:59.080 --> 01:14:03.130
to figure out how can we use
CRISPR for RNA diagnostics.

01:14:03.130 --> 01:14:05.660
This concept of using a CRISPR protein

01:14:05.660 --> 01:14:09.670
to detect certain RNA sequences isn't new,

01:14:09.670 --> 01:14:11.680
but the problem was that
previous approaches,

01:14:11.680 --> 01:14:13.070
even though they worked,

01:14:13.070 --> 01:14:14.580
they still need a higher sensitivity

01:14:14.580 --> 01:14:16.880
and also higher speed for
them to be really relevant

01:14:16.880 --> 01:14:19.143
and useful in clinical applications.

01:14:20.140 --> 01:14:22.320
And so we came up with this idea

01:14:22.320 --> 01:14:24.840
to try to more efficiently couple together

01:14:24.840 --> 01:14:27.620
two different CRISPR proteins in tandem,

01:14:27.620 --> 01:14:29.120
for really fast RNA sections.

01:14:29.120 --> 01:14:30.770
So essentially it takes this system

01:14:30.770 --> 01:14:33.630
that were kind of separate
systems before on their own,

01:14:33.630 --> 01:14:35.230
but combine them together

01:14:35.230 --> 01:14:37.100
to see if we could get a faster response.

01:14:37.100 --> 01:14:39.100
And this is one example
of kind of the signal

01:14:39.100 --> 01:14:39.933
that comes out.

01:14:39.933 --> 01:14:41.130
So basically we're looking for something

01:14:41.130 --> 01:14:44.543
that rises as you over time.

01:14:45.523 --> 01:14:48.350
And so we had to make
a couple modifications

01:14:48.350 --> 01:14:49.183
with this system,

01:14:49.183 --> 01:14:50.090
actually get it working well.

01:14:50.090 --> 01:14:51.830
So you can see on the left,

01:14:51.830 --> 01:14:53.650
this is a perfect example
actually of, you know,

01:14:53.650 --> 01:14:55.990
many people earlier, speaking of saying,

01:14:55.990 --> 01:14:57.430
sometimes things don't work.

01:14:57.430 --> 01:14:58.750
And you can see here on the left,

01:14:58.750 --> 01:15:01.170
we tested multiple sort of concentrations

01:15:01.170 --> 01:15:03.057
of the molecule that we wanted to detect

01:15:03.057 --> 01:15:05.080
and we don't get very good signal,

01:15:05.080 --> 01:15:06.920
but we found that actually

01:15:06.920 --> 01:15:10.110
by really looking at the
function of this one protein,

01:15:10.110 --> 01:15:12.890
that we could engineer its activator

01:15:12.890 --> 01:15:15.630
by putting this kind of
pink modification here

01:15:15.630 --> 01:15:17.300
to make it work better.

01:15:17.300 --> 01:15:20.170
And essentially by just
introducing that single change,

01:15:20.170 --> 01:15:22.410
you could see the
difference in signal here

01:15:22.410 --> 01:15:24.660
from almost close to baseline

01:15:24.660 --> 01:15:26.550
to this really robust response.

01:15:26.550 --> 01:15:29.850
And that actually increased
our sensitivity by 100 fold.

01:15:29.850 --> 01:15:31.760
And so it was just really
exciting to see this

01:15:31.760 --> 01:15:33.730
as kind of, you know,
these moments in science

01:15:33.730 --> 01:15:35.920
where a lot of times things
don't work the first time

01:15:35.920 --> 01:15:37.130
and second time and third time,

01:15:37.130 --> 01:15:38.318
but then when they do work that one time,

01:15:38.318 --> 01:15:40.730
it's just a really incredible feeling.

01:15:40.730 --> 01:15:42.070
And so we were able, ultimately,

01:15:42.070 --> 01:15:44.950
to sort of in addition
incorporate other improvements

01:15:44.950 --> 01:15:47.710
to get to a sensitivity that
was sort of clinically useful

01:15:47.710 --> 01:15:50.740
which is attomolar concentration rate,

01:15:50.740 --> 01:15:54.467
that's about 30 copies per
microliter of SARS-Cov-2

01:15:54.467 --> 01:15:56.283
RNA in about 20 minutes.

01:15:57.900 --> 01:16:01.560
And in addition, since we have
an on-campus for testing lab,

01:16:01.560 --> 01:16:04.760
we're able to actually take
some samples that were leftover

01:16:04.760 --> 01:16:06.160
from their lab,

01:16:06.160 --> 01:16:11.160
and see if we could use our
assay to detect SARS-CoV-2 RNA

01:16:11.700 --> 01:16:13.820
that were actually from patient samples.

01:16:13.820 --> 01:16:14.770
And what you can see here,

01:16:14.770 --> 01:16:15.987
so I won't go into too much detail

01:16:15.987 --> 01:16:17.600
but this black line is essentially,

01:16:17.600 --> 01:16:19.160
we're looking for a difference
between this black line

01:16:19.160 --> 01:16:20.600
and the pink line.

01:16:20.600 --> 01:16:21.433
And you can see here

01:16:21.433 --> 01:16:23.510
that you can see this above the pink line

01:16:23.510 --> 01:16:24.960
which means that it's positive.

01:16:24.960 --> 01:16:25.793
And again for here,

01:16:25.793 --> 01:16:28.110
this is actually with a lower viral load.

01:16:28.110 --> 01:16:30.280
So this number means that there's
less viral particles there

01:16:30.280 --> 01:16:32.170
there are RNA there,

01:16:32.170 --> 01:16:35.190
and it's still above but
it's closer to baseline.

01:16:35.190 --> 01:16:36.800
On the other hand, on negative samples,

01:16:36.800 --> 01:16:40.620
all show that it was below
this pink baseline here.

01:16:40.620 --> 01:16:42.760
So essentially we could
distinguish between positive

01:16:42.760 --> 01:16:45.310
and negative samples at this range

01:16:45.310 --> 01:16:48.030
of Ct values between eight and 29,

01:16:48.030 --> 01:16:50.790
which corresponds to the
early infectious stages

01:16:50.790 --> 01:16:51.750
of COVID-19.

01:16:51.750 --> 01:16:54.330
And that's really nice for us

01:16:54.330 --> 01:16:57.670
because essentially when you
want to control a pandemic

01:16:57.670 --> 01:16:59.050
it's not necessarily you have to have

01:16:59.050 --> 01:17:03.110
the absolute most sensitive
tests, in the world,

01:17:03.110 --> 01:17:04.960
actually what's more
important in some cases

01:17:04.960 --> 01:17:06.300
is be able to frequently test

01:17:06.300 --> 01:17:08.750
and catch people at the
most infectious stages

01:17:08.750 --> 01:17:11.210
when they're most likely to
spread it to other people.

01:17:11.210 --> 01:17:13.520
And so it was really nice for
us to see that in this range,

01:17:13.520 --> 01:17:15.330
between less than 29

01:17:15.330 --> 01:17:18.950
where most positives in
this kind of group work,

01:17:18.950 --> 01:17:21.270
we could actually able, we
were able to detect them

01:17:21.270 --> 01:17:24.783
with this technology
that we had developed.

01:17:25.844 --> 01:17:28.280
And so just to kind of conclude,

01:17:28.280 --> 01:17:31.483
basically we found that these
unrelated CRISPR nucleases

01:17:31.483 --> 01:17:33.510
that were from different sort of bacteria,

01:17:33.510 --> 01:17:36.810
different biological systems,

01:17:36.810 --> 01:17:38.500
could be really combined together

01:17:38.500 --> 01:17:40.700
for fast and sensitive RNA detection.

01:17:40.700 --> 01:17:43.700
And that makes it that simplicity also

01:17:43.700 --> 01:17:46.620
of being this kind of a single reaction

01:17:46.620 --> 01:17:49.390
makes it really amenable for rapid testing

01:17:49.390 --> 01:17:52.110
which is often approved
as point of care diagnosis

01:17:53.490 --> 01:17:55.990
compared to, for example,
traditional approaches,

01:17:55.990 --> 01:17:58.080
which require, you know centralized labs,

01:17:58.080 --> 01:18:00.960
a lot of equipment, trained personnel.

01:18:00.960 --> 01:18:02.500
And I think just to end with,

01:18:02.500 --> 01:18:04.920
I think again this sort
of goes toward the point

01:18:04.920 --> 01:18:07.670
that if you have an understanding
of how the molecules work

01:18:07.670 --> 01:18:09.370
to drive a process in life,

01:18:09.370 --> 01:18:12.730
that can really lead to
innovations that are valuable

01:18:12.730 --> 01:18:14.020
in biology research and medicine

01:18:14.020 --> 01:18:15.380
that's not just true for this,

01:18:15.380 --> 01:18:16.260
but also you can see

01:18:16.260 --> 01:18:18.690
in my mentor Jennifer
Doudna's work on CRISPR,

01:18:18.690 --> 01:18:21.310
that CRISPR is a natural bacterial system.

01:18:21.310 --> 01:18:22.590
Nobody ever thought, you know,

01:18:22.590 --> 01:18:24.470
it was not designed for genome editing,

01:18:24.470 --> 01:18:27.120
but nowadays it's been widely adopted

01:18:27.120 --> 01:18:29.240
for genome manipulation.

01:18:29.240 --> 01:18:30.770
And so I think I have to end

01:18:30.770 --> 01:18:32.350
by truly thanking the MB&amp;B program

01:18:32.350 --> 01:18:36.310
for really instilling that
appreciation for molecular events

01:18:36.310 --> 01:18:40.290
and also STARS II for this support.

01:18:40.290 --> 01:18:42.450
And of course mentors, Lynne Regan,

01:18:42.450 --> 01:18:44.720
Jennifer Doudna, Tom Rapoport,

01:18:44.720 --> 01:18:47.490
and also the great Doudna
Lab for their help.

01:18:47.490 --> 01:18:49.890
I'm happy to talk more
at the breakout sessions.

01:18:50.900 --> 01:18:52.750
- Thank you very much Tina.

01:18:52.750 --> 01:18:56.860
Our last alumni speaker
today is Elias Quijano.

01:18:56.860 --> 01:19:00.240
He's a current MD/PhD student
at Yale medical school

01:19:00.240 --> 01:19:03.000
and my STARS I graduate coordinator.

01:19:03.000 --> 01:19:04.560
Elias.

01:19:04.560 --> 01:19:05.980
- Hi everyone.

01:19:05.980 --> 01:19:07.590
Thanks for having me today.

01:19:07.590 --> 01:19:08.570
My name is Elias.

01:19:08.570 --> 01:19:10.180
I'm a, as Dean Chang mentioned,

01:19:10.180 --> 01:19:12.140
currently an MD/PhD student at Yale,

01:19:12.140 --> 01:19:15.853
but graduated from Yale
a long time ago in 2012.

01:19:16.961 --> 01:19:18.237
You know, I've been really fortunate

01:19:18.237 --> 01:19:20.840
to be part of the STARS program.

01:19:20.840 --> 01:19:23.870
You know, I remember my first day at Yale

01:19:23.870 --> 01:19:25.907
just feeling completely out of place.

01:19:25.907 --> 01:19:29.940
So I'm from a small town in
Florida, Hialeah, Florida.

01:19:29.940 --> 01:19:32.430
It's really small city in Miami.

01:19:32.430 --> 01:19:36.410
And growing up just like in
a Spanish speaking community

01:19:36.410 --> 01:19:37.790
with Spanish speaking parents,

01:19:37.790 --> 01:19:39.860
I never felt out of place.

01:19:39.860 --> 01:19:41.690
I always felt like I was home.

01:19:41.690 --> 01:19:45.160
And when I got to Yale,
that feeling of belonging

01:19:45.160 --> 01:19:48.040
was immediately swept away from me

01:19:48.040 --> 01:19:50.000
and I immediately fell out of place then.

01:19:50.000 --> 01:19:52.970
And I think many of
you here probably share

01:19:52.970 --> 01:19:54.270
with some of that feeling.

01:19:55.680 --> 01:19:58.210
And I remember, you know,
even the smallest things

01:19:58.210 --> 01:20:00.580
it made me feel like a foreigner.

01:20:00.580 --> 01:20:05.070
So one big thing in Florida
is wearing these silk shirts,

01:20:05.070 --> 01:20:06.360
these (indistinct).

01:20:06.360 --> 01:20:10.220
And I remember wearing one,
probably my first week at Yale.

01:20:10.220 --> 01:20:12.260
And you know, one of my
floor mates made, you know,

01:20:12.260 --> 01:20:14.870
some sort of a joke about my shirt.

01:20:14.870 --> 01:20:17.450
And I just remember immediately feeling

01:20:17.450 --> 01:20:19.210
like I didn't belong here.

01:20:19.210 --> 01:20:22.880
And I think, you know, early grades,

01:20:22.880 --> 01:20:24.840
you know, my first C at Yale

01:20:26.320 --> 01:20:29.070
compounded that feeling
of not belonging at Yale,

01:20:29.070 --> 01:20:30.993
feeling like I didn't belong

01:20:30.993 --> 01:20:34.530
and really, you know, that
first year I felt really lost.

01:20:34.530 --> 01:20:36.540
And I think serving currently

01:20:36.540 --> 01:20:39.520
as the graduate student
coordinator for STARS I,

01:20:39.520 --> 01:20:41.150
many students feel this way

01:20:41.150 --> 01:20:43.410
and it really doesn't matter
what your background is

01:20:43.410 --> 01:20:44.660
or where you're from,

01:20:44.660 --> 01:20:47.090
it's really hard to feel
like you belong here,

01:20:47.090 --> 01:20:50.290
especially if you're from a
underrepresented community

01:20:50.290 --> 01:20:52.453
or a first generation college student,

01:20:52.453 --> 01:20:54.150
it's really easy to feel lost

01:20:54.150 --> 01:20:57.820
and feel like you don't have
a community here at Yale.

01:20:57.820 --> 01:21:00.335
But I remember my sophomore year.

01:21:00.335 --> 01:21:03.820
I interacted with with
Dean Garcia at la casa.

01:21:03.820 --> 01:21:08.420
And she really pointed
me to the STARS program

01:21:08.420 --> 01:21:12.090
and really pointed me to
people who made me feel like

01:21:12.090 --> 01:21:13.825
like I did belong at Yale.

01:21:13.825 --> 01:21:15.320
And she was one of the first people

01:21:15.320 --> 01:21:16.990
who really introduced me to the program

01:21:16.990 --> 01:21:18.940
and introduced me to the faculty members

01:21:18.940 --> 01:21:19.773
that were part of STARS

01:21:19.773 --> 01:21:21.788
that had sort of voluntarily chosen

01:21:21.788 --> 01:21:23.530
to mentor students like myself

01:21:23.530 --> 01:21:27.390
which was really remarkable
to me at the time.

01:21:27.390 --> 01:21:28.990
And I think like many
of you have mentioned,

01:21:28.990 --> 01:21:33.390
you know, Dr. Black was
just an absolute force.

01:21:33.390 --> 01:21:38.390
I mean, you could sort of
be failing and messing up

01:21:39.340 --> 01:21:41.300
and not be on time for something

01:21:41.300 --> 01:21:43.010
and she was after you,
she was pushing you,

01:21:43.010 --> 01:21:44.580
she was asking you why
you weren't showing up

01:21:44.580 --> 01:21:45.920
to review sessions.

01:21:45.920 --> 01:21:47.630
You know, I remember one time,

01:21:47.630 --> 01:21:49.140
at the time when I was a student,

01:21:49.140 --> 01:21:51.880
she was hosting these nightly
review sessions for chemistry,

01:21:51.880 --> 01:21:53.360
and I think I missed it once.

01:21:53.360 --> 01:21:54.780
And she was like, "Why aren't you here?

01:21:54.780 --> 01:21:56.400
You're the student who
belongs here the most.

01:21:56.400 --> 01:21:57.533
You need the most help."

01:21:57.533 --> 01:22:00.410
And, you know, it was
that level of intensity

01:22:00.410 --> 01:22:03.720
and that level of like, sort
of looking out for students

01:22:03.720 --> 01:22:04.890
and chasing students down

01:22:04.890 --> 01:22:07.720
that I think is really really remarkable

01:22:07.720 --> 01:22:10.450
and really made Dr. Black

01:22:10.450 --> 01:22:12.590
stand out among all of
the faculty members here.

01:22:12.590 --> 01:22:15.428
You know she really chased after you.

01:22:15.428 --> 01:22:17.350
And the same was true for Dr. Moreno.

01:22:17.350 --> 01:22:20.960
So, you know, I ended
up doing STARS Summer,

01:22:20.960 --> 01:22:23.940
and Dr. Moreno had a similar
energy and a similar force,

01:22:23.940 --> 01:22:27.180
but you know, she really taught
me how to succeed in science

01:22:27.180 --> 01:22:29.200
and how to succeed in STEM.

01:22:29.200 --> 01:22:32.810
And that to me was a
really really remarkable

01:22:32.810 --> 01:22:34.480
just having someone
who was so accomplished

01:22:34.480 --> 01:22:36.480
in her own field, in her own right,

01:22:36.480 --> 01:22:39.840
take the time to mentor me and shepherd me

01:22:39.840 --> 01:22:43.580
and just teach me what I
needed to know from, you know,

01:22:43.580 --> 01:22:46.840
how to write an email that was
appropriate to a professor,

01:22:46.840 --> 01:22:49.240
to how to design a slide that didn't look

01:22:49.240 --> 01:22:51.120
like the example we saw
a little bit earlier,

01:22:51.120 --> 01:22:54.410
and I know we all make jokes
about that, but it's true.

01:22:54.410 --> 01:22:56.660
I mean, you know, the number
one thing you're doing

01:22:56.660 --> 01:22:59.850
as a graduate student or
someone in STEM or in any field

01:22:59.850 --> 01:23:02.800
is communicating and being
able to communicate effectively

01:23:02.800 --> 01:23:05.990
and clearly is just so invaluable.

01:23:05.990 --> 01:23:07.450
And it's a bit of a softer skill

01:23:07.450 --> 01:23:10.140
but one that I really credit STARS with,

01:23:10.140 --> 01:23:13.670
you know, I think STARS for
me was really the first time

01:23:13.670 --> 01:23:15.950
that, not only was I taught how to succeed

01:23:15.950 --> 01:23:19.020
but I was also taught to never give up.

01:23:19.020 --> 01:23:20.520
I think deep down inside,

01:23:20.520 --> 01:23:23.023
I always knew I wanted to pursue STEM.

01:23:24.330 --> 01:23:26.950
I didn't have parents that were scientists

01:23:26.950 --> 01:23:29.340
or went to college for that matter,

01:23:29.340 --> 01:23:31.290
but, you know, the STARS faculty members

01:23:31.290 --> 01:23:33.418
really taught me to kind of pursue that

01:23:33.418 --> 01:23:35.060
and never give up on that

01:23:35.060 --> 01:23:39.836
and really seek mentors that
could help me along the way.

01:23:39.836 --> 01:23:43.040
And, you know, that was
extremely invaluable for me.

01:23:43.040 --> 01:23:46.220
So since that time, as a STARS student,

01:23:46.220 --> 01:23:47.960
I, you know, I ended up doing STARS Summer

01:23:47.960 --> 01:23:49.133
as well as STARS II.

01:23:50.050 --> 01:23:50.883
One thing I'll mention

01:23:50.883 --> 01:23:53.060
that someone actually said
a little earlier today,

01:23:53.060 --> 01:23:55.100
is that in addition to the mentorship,

01:23:55.100 --> 01:23:57.700
one key piece of the STARS Program

01:23:57.700 --> 01:24:00.010
was the financial support that I received.

01:24:00.010 --> 01:24:04.230
So, at the time when I was
doing STARS I and STARS II,

01:24:04.230 --> 01:24:06.960
I had the opportunity to
go to multiple conferences

01:24:06.960 --> 01:24:09.370
and present multiple posters and talks,

01:24:09.370 --> 01:24:12.130
and it was because the STARS
program pay for my lodging

01:24:12.130 --> 01:24:13.440
and pay for my registration.

01:24:13.440 --> 01:24:15.560
I mean, there was absolutely no way

01:24:15.560 --> 01:24:17.250
I was going to be able to take advantage

01:24:17.250 --> 01:24:18.400
of those opportunities

01:24:18.400 --> 01:24:21.840
if STARS wasn't financing
those opportunities for me.

01:24:21.840 --> 01:24:23.690
And that was really, really invaluable,

01:24:23.690 --> 01:24:25.380
even with STARS II.

01:24:25.380 --> 01:24:27.820
You know, the opportunity
to get paid to do research,

01:24:27.820 --> 01:24:30.750
to both further your
intellectual development

01:24:30.750 --> 01:24:32.020
and your research skills,

01:24:32.020 --> 01:24:35.090
while also generating the
income that perhaps you need

01:24:35.090 --> 01:24:36.400
because you're sending money home

01:24:36.400 --> 01:24:38.560
or you're paying for your
own books, or, you know,

01:24:38.560 --> 01:24:41.060
paying for your own day-to-day expenses,

01:24:41.060 --> 01:24:45.080
it's really something that,
you know, I sort of look back

01:24:45.080 --> 01:24:46.800
and I think many of us feel the same way.

01:24:46.800 --> 01:24:50.210
You know, I really credit
STARS with sort of saving me

01:24:50.210 --> 01:24:53.370
in many ways and putting
me on the right track.

01:24:53.370 --> 01:24:54.330
But since that time,

01:24:54.330 --> 01:24:57.070
I went on to start an
MD/PhD program at Yale,

01:24:57.070 --> 01:25:00.460
thankfully, to most of my STARS mentors,

01:25:00.460 --> 01:25:03.710
including Dean Chang and, you know,

01:25:03.710 --> 01:25:06.930
have recently started my own biotech

01:25:06.930 --> 01:25:08.520
and just closed our series A.

01:25:08.520 --> 01:25:12.230
So, you know, I say
that not to really brag,

01:25:12.230 --> 01:25:13.450
but just to say that, you know,

01:25:13.450 --> 01:25:17.600
I went from getting Cs in
chemistry and you know,

01:25:17.600 --> 01:25:20.630
virtually feeling out of a university

01:25:20.630 --> 01:25:22.890
to now achieving some level of success.

01:25:22.890 --> 01:25:24.900
And it really wasn't
something I did on my own.

01:25:24.900 --> 01:25:26.850
It was really something
I did because of STARS

01:25:26.850 --> 01:25:28.790
and through the support
I received from STARS,

01:25:28.790 --> 01:25:31.970
So, you know, I'm just really
grateful to the students

01:25:31.970 --> 01:25:33.540
I've been able to mentor through STARS,

01:25:33.540 --> 01:25:36.280
but especially to the
individuals who have mentored me.

01:25:36.280 --> 01:25:38.520
I mean, it's really been
an invaluable process

01:25:38.520 --> 01:25:39.830
and experience for myself.

01:25:39.830 --> 01:25:41.720
And, you know, I'm just really grateful

01:25:41.720 --> 01:25:42.780
to be here with you all

01:25:42.780 --> 01:25:46.030
and to see that so many people
benefited from this program

01:25:46.030 --> 01:25:48.630
and decided to give back
by being here today.

01:25:48.630 --> 01:25:49.830
So thank you all

01:25:49.830 --> 01:25:52.170
and thank you Dean Chang
for organizing all of this

01:25:52.170 --> 01:25:53.693
and putting us all together.

01:25:54.860 --> 01:25:56.020
- Thank you very much Elias

01:25:56.020 --> 01:25:58.983
and thank you to all the
speakers in the morning session.

